In the early hours of 31 August 1997, [[Diana, Princess of Wales]], died from injuries sustained in a car crash in the [[Pont de l'Alma]] tunnel in [[Paris]], France. [[Dodi Fayed]], Diana's partner, and the chauffeur Henri Paul were both found dead inside the car. Diana's bodyguard, [[Trevor Rees-Jones (bodyguard)|Trevor Rees-Jones]], was the only survivor of the crash, albeit seriously injured. The subsequent investigation into the crash remained controversial for decades, with many challenging the official narrative of Diana's death.<ref name="theories"/>

In 1999, a French investigation concluded that Paul, who was intoxicated and under the influence of prescription drugs, lost control of the vehicle at high speed. The report held him solely responsible for the crash. Paul was the deputy head of security at the [[Hôtel Ritz Paris]] and had earlier confronted paparazzi waiting for Diana and Fayed outside the hotel.<ref name="days-shook">{{cite episode |title=The Coronation of Elizabeth II/The Death of Diana |series=Days That Shook the World |series-link=Days that Shook the World |credits=Director: David Bartlett, Executive Producer: David Upshal |work=BBC News }}</ref> [[Antidepressant]]s and traces of an anti-psychotic found in his blood may have compounded his impairment.<ref name=autogenerated1>{{cite news |last1=Nundy |first1=Julian |last2=Graves |first2=David |title=Diana crash caused by chauffeur, says report |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml?html=/archive/1999/09/04/ndi04.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021113190313/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml?html=%2Farchive%2F1999%2F09%2F04%2Fndi04.html |archive-date=13 November 2002 |url-access=subscription }}</ref> In 2008, a British inquest, [[Operation Paget]], returned a verdict of [[unlawful killing]], citing the grossly negligent driving; of both Paul and the pursuing paparazzi.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rayner |first=Gordon |date=7 April 2008 |title=Diana jury blames paparazzi and Henri Paul for her 'unlawful killing' |work=Daily Telegraph |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584160/Diana-jury-blames-paparazzi-and-Henri-Paul-for-her-unlawful-killing.html |url-status=live |access-date=12 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180425124749/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584160/Diana-jury-blames-paparazzi-and-Henri-Paul-for-her-unlawful-killing.html |archive-date=25 April 2018 |url-access=subscription }}</ref> While initial media reports suggested Rees-Jones survived because he was wearing a [[seat belt]], later investigations confirmed that none of the car's occupants was wearing seat belts.<ref name="Sky">{{cite news |url=https://news.sky.com/story/trevor-rees-jones-what-happened-to-the-sole-survivor-of-dianas-crash-11005463 |title=Trevor Rees-Jones: What happened to the sole survivor of Diana's crash |work=Sky News |first=Connor |last=Sephton |date=28 August 2017 |access-date=28 November 2020 |archive-date=19 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201119170154/https://news.sky.com/story/trevor-rees-jones-what-happened-to-the-sole-survivor-of-dianas-crash-11005463 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Diana was 36 years old at the time of her death.<ref name="johnston98">{{cite book |first=Carla B. |last=Johnston |title=Global News Access: The Impact of New Communications Technologies |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aAlJn5ZpvGcC&pg=PA1 |year=1998 |publisher=Greenwood Publishing Group |isbn=978-0-275-95774-2 |page=1 |access-date=7 September 2019 |archive-date=19 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200219094724/https://books.google.com/books?id=aAlJn5ZpvGcC&pg=PA1 |url-status=live }}</ref> Her death prompted an international outpouring of grief,<ref name="WorldMourns"/> and [[Funeral of Diana, Princess of Wales|her televised funeral]] was watched by an estimated 2.5{{nbsp}}billion people worldwide.<ref name="TelevisionAudience"/>

==Circumstances==

===Events preceding the crash===
[[File:Mercedes W140 front 20070609.jpg|thumb|[[Mercedes-Benz W140|Mercedes-Benz S 280 saloon (W140]]<ref name="washingtonpost-belts0903">{{cite news |last1=Brown |first1=Warren |date=3 September 1997 |title=Seat Belts Might Have Saved Diana |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/inatl/longterm/diana/stories/belts0903.htm |access-date=1 September 2022 |archive-date=10 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170910062006/http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-srv/inatl/longterm/diana/stories/belts0903.htm |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> [[Mercedes-Benz S-Class|S-Class)]], similar to the one involved in the crash<ref name="mercedesblog-written-off"/><ref name="telegraph.co.uk-death-trap">{{cite news |last1=Mulholland |first1=Rory |date=31 May 2017 |title=Car Princess Diana died in was 'death trap' that had been written off and rebuilt, according to new report |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2017/05/31/limo-princess-diana-died-death-trap-had-written-rebuilt-according/ |access-date=1 September 2022 |archive-date=1 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901023038/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2017/05/31/limo-princess-diana-died-death-trap-had-written-rebuilt-according/ |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name="independent.co.uk-car-dangerous-rebuilt-wreck">{{cite news |last1=Kentish |first1=Benjamin |date=31 May 2017 |title=Car Princess Diana was travelling in when killed was a 'hugely dangerous' rebuilt wreck, say reports |language=en |work=[[The Independent]] |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/princess-diana-paris-car-killed-in-travelling-hugely-dangerous-rebuilt-wreck-reports-henri-paul-pascal-rostain-mercedes-a7765356.html |access-date=1 September 2022 |archive-date=1 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901023038/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/princess-diana-paris-car-killed-in-travelling-hugely-dangerous-rebuilt-wreck-reports-henri-paul-pascal-rostain-mercedes-a7765356.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="goodhousekeeping/a44438"/><ref name="news.bbc.co.uk/2493315-OTD">{{cite news |date=1 September 1997 |title=1997: Diana driver was 'drunk and speeding' |work=[[BBC News]] |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/september/1/newsid_2493000/2493315.stm |access-date=1 September 2022 |archive-date=13 February 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080213142043/http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/september/1/newsid_2493000/2493315.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>|left]]
On Saturday, 30 August 1997, Diana travelled from [[Olbia Airport]], [[Sardinia]], on a private jet and arrived at [[Le Bourget Airport]]<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk-Timeline">{{cite news |title=Timeline: How Diana died |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/6217366.stm |access-date=1 September 2022 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=14 December 2006 |archive-date=14 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220914192953/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/6217366.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> in Paris with Egyptian film producer [[Dodi Fayed]], son of businessman [[Mohamed Al-Fayed]].<ref name=merrin99>{{cite journal |last=Merrin |first=William |title=Crash, bang, wallop! What a picture! The death of Diana and the media |journal=Mortality |year=1999 |volume=4 |issue=1 |pages=41–62 |doi=10.1080/713685965 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/ending1.html |title=What Were You Doing When ...<!-- ellipsis in the original -->? |work=BBC News |access-date=18 April 2021 |archive-date=23 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201123152458/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/ending1.html |url-status=live }}</ref> They had stopped there en route to London, having spent the preceding nine days together on board his father's yacht ''Jonikal'' on the [[French Riviera|French]] and [[Italian Riviera]].{{sfn|Cohen|2005|pp=47,51}} They had intended to stay there for the night. His father was the owner of the [[Hôtel Ritz Paris]] and resided in an apartment on Rue Arsène Houssaye, a short distance from the hotel; just off the [[Champs-Élysées|Avenue des Champs Elysées]].{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=277}}

Henri Paul (3 July 1956 – 31 August 1997), deputy head of security at the Ritz, had been instructed to drive the hired black 1994<ref name="mercedesblog-written-off">{{cite web |last1=Luchian |first1=Elena |title=The Mercedes Lady Diana died in had been {{sic |nolink=y |reason=error in source|writen}}-off 3 years before the crash |url=https://mercedesblog.com/the-mercedes-lady-diana-died-in-had-been-written-off/ |website=MercedesBlog |access-date=1 September 2022 |date=1 June 2017 |archive-date=6 June 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170606012400/https://mercedesblog.com/the-mercedes-lady-diana-died-in-had-been-written-off/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="telegraph.co.uk-death-trap"/><ref name="independent.co.uk-car-dangerous-rebuilt-wreck"/><ref name="goodhousekeeping/a44438">{{cite news |last1=Picard |first1=Caroline |title=Original Owner Says Car From Diana's Fatal Crash Was a "Dangerous" Wreck |url=https://www.goodhousekeeping.com/life/news/a44438/princess-diana-car-crash/ |access-date=1 September 2022 |work=[[Good Housekeeping]] |date=1 June 2017 |language=en-us |archive-date=1 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901023038/https://www.goodhousekeeping.com/life/news/a44438/princess-diana-car-crash/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[armoured]]<ref name="news.bbc.co.uk/2493315-OTD"/> [[Mercedes-Benz W140|Mercedes-Benz S 280 saloon registered 688LTV75 (W140]]<ref name="washingtonpost-belts0903"/> [[Mercedes-Benz S-Class|S-Class)]] in order to elude the [[paparazzi]];<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/6217366.stm |title=Timeline: How Diana died |date=14 December 2006 |work=BBC News |access-date=13 October 2008 |archive-date=11 January 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090111225857/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/6217366.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> a [[decoy]] vehicle left the Ritz first from the main entrance on [[Place Vendôme]], attracting a throng of photographers. Diana and Fayed then departed from the hotel's rear entrance,<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457275/ |title=Car Crash Kills Princess Diana |date=31 August 1997 |work=St. Louis Post-Dispatch |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045235/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457275/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Rue Cambon, at around 00:20 on 31 August [[Central European Summer Time|CEST]] (22:20 on 30 August [[UTC]]), heading for the apartment in Rue Arsène Houssaye. They did this to avoid the nearly 30 photographers waiting in front of the hotel.<ref name=":3" /> Diana and Fayed were the rear passengers;<!--by Operation Paget Report, but French Diana crash inquiry report: "police and rescue services discovered a black Mercedes vehicle, type S280, registration number 680 LTV75." Registration number 680 or 688 in French report ??--> [[Trevor Rees-Jones (bodyguard)|Trevor Rees-Jones]], a member of the Fayed family's personal protection team, was in the front passenger seat.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=59}} None of the vehicle's occupants was wearing seat belts.{{efn|Although there are conflicting reports (such as [https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/88special/politics97/diana/bodyguard.html BBC]{{dead link|date=September 2025|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}} and [https://web.archive.org/web/20080110224025/http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/21/diana.investigation/ CNN]), the investigation [[Operation Paget]] report notes "Operation Paget's view is that none of the seat belts were being worn at the time of the impact, including that of Trevor Rees-Jones. From the nature of marks found on his seat belt, it is considered unlikely that he was even in the process of attempting to put it on at all at the time of the crash." (Page 421) [https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/news/nol/shared/bsp/hi/pdfs/14_12_06_diana_report.pdf].}} After leaving the Rue Cambon and crossing the [[Place de la Concorde]], they drove along [[Cours la Reine]] and Cours Albert 1er – the embankment road along the right bank of the [[River Seine]] – into the Place de l'Alma underpass.

===The crash===
At 00:23 [[Central European Summer Time|CEST]] (22:23 [[UTC]]), Paul lost control of the car at the entrance to the [[Pont de l'Alma]] underpass. The car reportedly struck a white [[Fiat Uno#First generation (1983)|Fiat Uno]], swerved to the left of the two-lane carriageway and collided head-on with the 13th column that supported the roof.<ref>{{cite news |title=Not finding white Fiat Uno in tunnel on night of Diana's death a 'frustration,' crash investigators say |url=https://www.fox6now.com/news/not-finding-white-fiat-uno-in-tunnel-on-night-of-dianas-death-a-frustration-crash-investigators-say |website=FOX6 |date=31 August 2022 |access-date=14 October 2022 |archive-date=14 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221014184850/https://www.fox6now.com/news/not-finding-white-fiat-uno-in-tunnel-on-night-of-dianas-death-a-frustration-crash-investigators-say |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Report 'dispels Diana theories' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3426309.stm |work=BBC News |date=24 January 2004 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=12 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612115958/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3426309.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> The Mercedes driven by Paul was travelling at an estimated speed of {{convert|105|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}<ref>[[#pagetreport|John Stevens, Baron Stevens of Kirkwhelpington]] p. 41.</ref>{{snd}}more than twice the {{cvt|50|km/h}} speed limit of the tunnel. The vehicle then spun, hit the stone wall of the tunnel backwards and finally came to a stop. The impact caused substantial damage, particularly to the front half of the vehicle, as there was no [[Guard rail#Automotive safety|guard rail]] to mitigate collisions.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=97}} Witnesses arriving shortly after the crash reported smoke.<ref name=":2">{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13456217/ |title=Princess Diana Dies in Car Crash |date=31 August 1997 |work=[[Quad-City Times]] |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831043245/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13456217/ |url-status=live }}</ref> They also reported that photographers on motorcycles "swarmed the Mercedes sedan before it entered the tunnel".<ref name=":6">{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457985/ |title=Police Seize Film |date=1 September 1997 |work=The Central New Jersey Home News |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045241/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457985/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Aftermath===
[[File:Alma flamme de la Liberté + tunnel.jpg|thumb|Pont de l'Alma Tunnel west entrance, 2007, showing pillars and lack of guard rails]]
[[File:West entrance to the Pont de l'Alma tunnel in Paris, where Princess Diana died.jpg|thumb|West entrance to the Pont de l'Alma tunnel in Paris (2023)]]
The photographers had been driving slower and were some distance behind the Mercedes. When they reached the scene, some rushed to help, trying to open the doors and help the victims, while some of them took pictures.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=71}} Police arrived around 10 minutes after the crash at 00:30,{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=71}} and an ambulance was on site five minutes later, according to witnesses.<ref name=":4">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457637/ |title=Sound Drew People; Sight Was Grisly |last=Kornblut |first=Anne E. |date=1 September 1997 |work=The Cincinnati Enquirer |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045233/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457637/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[France Info (radio network)|France Info radio]] reported that one photographer was beaten by witnesses who were horrified by the scene.<ref name=":6" /> Five of the photographers were arrested directly.<ref name=":2" /> Later, two others were detained and around 20 rolls of film were taken directly from the photographers<ref name=":6" /> while police later [[vehicle impoundment|impounded]] their vehicles.<ref name=":6" /> Firefighters arrived at the scene to help remove the victims.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/princess-diana-wales-20th-death-anniversary-fireman-last-words-paris-crash-a7921306.html |title=Fireman who treated Princess Diana after the crash reveals her last words |date=31 August 2017 |newspaper=The Independent |access-date=31 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831230850/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/princess-diana-wales-20th-death-anniversary-fireman-last-words-paris-crash-a7921306.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Rees-Jones sustained multiple serious facial injuries and a head contusion, but was conscious.<ref name=":8">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458498/ |title=Car's Speedometer Stuck at 121 MPH |last=Kole |first=William J. |date=1 September 1997 |work=Honolulu Star-Bulletin |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045229/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458498/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The front airbags had functioned normally.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/53752.stm |title=Diana crash airbags puzzle |date=5 February 1998 |work=BBC News |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831002957/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/53752.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Some media reports claimed that Rees-Jones was the only occupant wearing a seat belt at the time of the crash, but it was later determined that "no one inside the car had been wearing" one.<ref name="Sky" /> Diana was sitting in the rear passenger seat on the right-hand side and was critically injured, but she also remained conscious.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=71}}<ref name="Time-Report" /> The crash mostly affected the right-hand side of her body, indicating that Diana was sitting sideways in her seat at the time of impact.<ref name="Guardian-Nov2007">{{cite news |url=https://theguardian.com/uk/2007/nov/26/monarchy |title=Diana's body showed no signs of pregnancy, says pathologist |work=The Guardian |first=James |last=Orr |date=26 November 2007 |access-date=4 September 2022 |archive-date=4 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220904152234/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2007/nov/26/monarchy |url-status=live }}</ref> Her ribs and arm were fractured and her right collar bone was dislocated, and Diana suffered from swelling and bruising to the brain.<ref name="Guardian-Nov2007" /> She was reported to murmur repeatedly, "Oh my God", and after the photographers and other helpers were pushed away by police, "Leave me alone."<ref name="Time-Report">{{cite news |title=Special Report: Princess Diana, 1961–1997 |url=http://www.time.com/time/daily/special/diana/timeline/augsept97/10.html |newspaper=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |access-date=1 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090430123013/http://www.time.com/time/daily/special/diana/timeline/augsept97/10.html |archive-date=30 April 2009 }}</ref> In June 2007, the [[Channel 4]] documentary ''Diana: The Witnesses in the Tunnel'' claimed that the first person to touch Diana was off-duty physician Frederic Mailliez,<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.channel4.com/culture/microsites/D/diana/ |title=Diana: The Witnesses in the Tunnel |work=Channel4 |access-date=30 April 2011 |archive-date=3 July 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090703134445/http://www.channel4.com/culture/microsites/D/diana/ |url-status=live }}</ref> who chanced upon the scene. Mailliez reported that Diana had no visible injuries but was in shock.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=126}} She was reported to have been extremely disturbed and removed an intravenous drip while shouting incoherently.<ref name="Times-Nov2007"/> After being sedated and removed from the car at 01:00, Diana went into [[cardiac arrest]], but her heart started beating again following external [[cardiopulmonary resuscitation]].<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/culture/news/french-firefighter-reveals-princess-dianas-last-words-w500636 |title=Firefighter Reveals Princess Diana's Last Words 20 Years After Death |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]] |date=31 August 2017 |access-date=31 August 2017 |archive-date=1 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170901000039/http://www.rollingstone.com/culture/news/french-firefighter-reveals-princess-dianas-last-words-w500636 |url-status=live }}</ref> Diana was moved to the ambulance at 01:18, left the scene at 01:41, and arrived at the [[Pitié-Salpêtrière Hospital]] at 02:06.<ref>[[#pagetreport|John Stevens, Baron Stevens of Kirkwhelpington]] pp. 525–527.</ref>

Fayed was in the rear passenger seat on the left-hand side and was pronounced dead at the scene shortly afterwards.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1570885/Princess-Diana-watched-lover-Dodi-dying.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220111/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1570885/Princess-Diana-watched-lover-Dodi-dying.html |archive-date=11 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Princess Diana 'watched lover Dodi dying' |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |date=29 November 2007 |access-date=30 August 2017}}{{cbignore }}</ref> Paul was also pronounced dead at the scene on removal from the wreckage.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=71}} Both were taken directly to the Institut Médico-Légal (IML), the Paris mortuary.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=177}} Paul was later found to have a blood alcohol level of 180{{nbs}}milligrams per 100{{nbs}}millilitres of blood,{{efn|This was the result of the last of three blood tests conducted by 9 September 1997. The first and second tests recorded levels of 175mg and 187mg of alcohol per 100ml of blood respectively.<ref name= Lichfield1>{{cite news |last=Lichfield |first=John |title=Third test confirms Diana's driver was over alcohol limit |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/third-test-confirms-diana-s-driver-was-over-alcohol-limit-1238313.html |newspaper=The Independent |date=9 September 1997 |access-date=31 March 2024 |archive-date=31 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240331090613/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/third-test-confirms-diana-s-driver-was-over-alcohol-limit-1238313.html |url-status=live }}</ref> At that time, the legal limit in France for a driver was 50mg of alcohol per 100ml of blood.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/01/diana.french.probe.update/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160429144018/http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/01/diana.french.probe.update/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=29 April 2016 |title=Prosecutor: Diana's driver legally drunk |date=1 September 1997 |website=CNN |access-date=25 July 2024 }}</ref> A pathologist retained by the Fayed family criticised the methodology of the first two tests but the French authorities rejected this criticism.<ref name= Lichfield1/> Allegations were reported in news media in 2006 that the blood samples tested were not those of Paul. The coroner at Diana's British inquest in 2008 told the jury that the DNA evidence confirming that the samples belonged to Paul were not conclusive. Nevertheless, the jury, in its verdict, determined that Paul's [[drunk driving]] had contributed to the crash.<ref>{{cite news |last=Balakrishnan |first=Angela |title=Pregnancy rumours, MI6 plots and Henri Paul |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2008/apr/07/diana.monarchy1 |newspaper=The Guardian |date=7 April 2008 |access-date=31 March 2024 |archive-date=26 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200226014432/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2008/apr/07/diana.monarchy1 |url-status=live }}</ref>}} or 3.6 times the legal limit{{efn|Set in France at the time at 50{{nbs}}milligrams per 100{{nbs}}milliliters of blood.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/01/diana.french.probe.update/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160429144018/http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/01/diana.french.probe.update/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=29 April 2016 |title=Prosecutor: Diana's driver legally drunk |date=1 September 1997 |website=CNN |access-date=19 January 2026 }}</ref>}} in France.<ref name= Lichfield1/>

Diana's injuries were extensive, and resuscitation attempts were unsuccessful, including [[internal cardiac massage]]. Her heart had been displaced to the right side of the chest, which tore the upper left [[pulmonary vein]] and the [[pericardium]].<ref name="Times-Nov2007">{{cite news |url=https://www.thetimes.com/travel/destinations/europe-travel/france/paris/agitated-diana-ripped-out-drip-after-crash-fsxf8cj6xtn |title=Agitated Diana 'ripped out drip' after crash |work=[[The Times]] |first=Hannah |last=Strange |date=19 November 2007 |access-date=4 September 2022 |archive-date=4 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220904155330/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/agitated-diana-ripped-out-drip-after-crash-fsxf8cj6xtn |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Diana died at the hospital at 04:00.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=133}}<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.emergency.com/dianaded.htm |title=Series of Real-Time Reports involving the tragic death of Diana, Princess of Wales |website=emergency.com |year=1997 |access-date=13 October 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080507233601/http://www.emergency.com/dianaded.htm |archive-date=7 May 2008 }}</ref> Anaesthetist Bruno Riou announced her death at 06:00 at a news conference held at the hospital.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457080/ |title=Fatal End to Fairy Tale |date=31 August 1997 |work=Palladium-Item |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831042511/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457080/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Later that morning, French Prime Minister [[Lionel Jospin]] and Interior Minister [[Jean-Pierre Chevènement]] visited the hospital.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1485723/Secret-papers-recall-Dianas-final-hours.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220111/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1485723/Secret-papers-recall-Dianas-final-hours.html |archive-date=11 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Secret papers recall Diana's final hours |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |date=16 March 2005 |access-date=30 August 2017}}{{cbignore }}</ref> At around 17:00, Diana's former husband, then-Prince [[Charles III|Charles]], and her two older sisters, [[Lady Sarah McCorquodale]] and [[Lady Jane Fellowes]], arrived in Paris.{{sfn|Cohen|2005|p=143}} The group visited the hospital along with French president [[Jacques Chirac]] and thanked the doctors for trying to save her life.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467393/ |title=Diana: World Mourns as Princess's Body Returns Home |date=1 September 1997 |work=The Journal News |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025252/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467393/the-journal-news/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Charles accompanied Diana's body to the UK later the same day.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467272/ |title=The World Mourns a Princess |last=Johnson |first=Maureen |date=1 September 1997 |work=Hartford Courant |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025252/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467272/hartford-courant/ |url-status=live }}</ref> They departed from [[Vélizy – Villacoublay Air Base]] and landed at [[RAF Northolt]], and a bearer party from the [[Queen's Colour Squadron]] transferred her coffin to the hearse. The coffin was draped with the [[Royal Standard of the United Kingdom|royal standard]] with an [[Ermine (heraldry)|ermine]] border. Her body was finally taken to the Hammersmith and Fulham mortuary in London for a post-mortem examination later that day.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/women/life/beach-bbqs-dodi-rows-bodyguards-engagement-ring-truth-last-week/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170827061313/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/women/life/beach-bbqs-dodi-rows-bodyguards-engagement-ring-truth-last-week/ |archive-date=27 August 2017 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Beach BBQs with Dodi, rows with bodyguards and that 'engagement ring'...<!-- ellipsis in the original --> The truth about the last week of Princess Diana's life |first=Martyn |last=Gregory |date=24 August 2017 |access-date=29 May 2018 |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph}}{{cbignore }}</ref>

Initial media reports stated that Diana's car had collided with the pillar at {{convert|190|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}, and that the speedometer's needle had jammed at that position.<ref name=":8" /> It was later announced that the car's speed upon collision was {{cvt|95|-|110|km/h}}, about twice as fast as the speed limit of {{convert|50|km/h|mph|abbr=on}}. In 1999, a French investigation concluded that the Mercedes had come into contact with a white [[Fiat Uno]] in the tunnel.<ref name="Gregory">Martyn Gregory [https://books.google.com/books?id=vZPJjwm6ITMC&pg=PT70 ''Diana: The Last Days''] {{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200716001946/https://books.google.com/books?id=vZPJjwm6ITMC&pg=PT70 |date=16 July 2020 }}, Random House, 2010, p.70</ref> The driver of the Fiat was never conclusively traced, although many believed that the driver was [[Conspiracy theories about the death of Diana, Princess of Wales#Le Van Thanh|Le Van Thanh]]. Thanh was questioned by French detectives in 1997, who ruled him out as a suspect but friends and family members have noted inconsistencies in his story. Thanh has since refused interviews or inquiries from investigators.<ref name="Tel-Thanh">{{Cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584166/Inquest-shed-light-on-mystery-of-white-Fiat-Uno.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584166/Inquest-shed-light-on-mystery-of-white-Fiat-Uno.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Inquest shed light on mystery of white Fiat Uno |work=The Daily Telegraph |first=Gordon |last=Rayner |date=7 April 2008 |access-date=23 October 2018}}{{cbignore }}</ref> The specific vehicle was not identified.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584166/Inquest-shed-light-on-mystery-of-white-Fiat-Uno.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220111/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584166/Inquest-shed-light-on-mystery-of-white-Fiat-Uno.html |archive-date=11 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Inquest shed light on mystery of white Fiat Uno |date=7 April 2008 |last1=Reporter |first1=Gordon Rayner}}{{cbignore }}</ref><ref name="Gregory"/>

British Foreign Secretary [[Robin Cook]] remarked that, if the crash had been caused in part by being hounded by paparazzi, it would be "doubly tragic".<ref name=":3" /> Diana's younger brother, the [[Charles Spencer, 9th Earl Spencer|Earl Spencer]], also blamed tabloid media for her death.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457747/ |title=Right to Privacy Under Scrutiny |date=1 September 1997 |work=The Springfield News-Leader |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831042510/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457747/ |url-status=live }}</ref> An 18-month French judicial investigation concluded in 1999 that the crash was caused by Paul, who lost control at high speed while intoxicated.<ref name=autogenerated1/> None of the photographers was charged.<ref name="bbc-onthisday-2510615">{{cite news |title=1997: Princess Diana dies in Paris crash |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/august/31/newsid_2510000/2510615.stm |access-date=1 September 2022 |work=ON THIS DAY [[BBC]] |date=31 August 1997 |archive-date=24 January 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080124004738/http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/august/31/newsid_2510000/2510615.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Mourning ==
Members of the public were invited to sign a book of condolence at [[St James's Palace]].<ref name=":10">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13465881/ |title=Funeral Said 'Unique for a Unique Person' |date=2 September 1997 |work=Press and Sun |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025252/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13465881/press-and-sun-bulletin/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A book of condolence was also set up by the [[Embassy of the United Kingdom, Washington, D.C.|British embassy in the US]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.aparchive.com/metadata/youtube/a2981ae30370ea5f39cc4d6d431b2c57 |title=USA: British Embassy Opens Book of Condolences for Princess Diana |agency=Associated Press |date=1 September 1997 |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417162146/http://www.aparchive.com/metadata/youtube/a2981ae30370ea5f39cc4d6d431b2c57 |url-status=live }}</ref> All 11,000 light bulbs at [[Harrods]] department store, owned by Mohamed Al-Fayed, were turned off and not switched on again until after the funeral.<ref name=":10" /> Throughout the night, members of the [[Women's Royal Voluntary Service]] and the [[Salvation Army]] provided support for people queuing along [[The Mall, London|the Mall]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mourn.html |title=Public Mourning Continues – Royal Family 'Deeply Touched' |work=BBC News |access-date=11 November 2007 |archive-date=25 February 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090225091115/http://www.bbc.co.uk/politics97/diana/mourn.html |url-status=live }}</ref> More than one million bouquets were left at her London residence, [[Kensington Palace]],<ref>{{cite book |title=Diana Princess of Wales Paper Doll: The Charity Auction Dresses |last=Tierney |first=Tom |publisher=Courier Corporation |year=1997 |isbn=978-0-486-40015-0 |page=1989 }}</ref> while at her family's estate of [[Althorp]] the public was asked to stop bringing flowers as the volume of both visitors and flowers in the surrounding roads was said to be causing a threat to public safety.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.birminghammail.co.uk/news/midlands-news/19th-anniversary-death-diana-princess-11821657 |title=19th anniversary of death of Diana, Princess of Wales |date=31 August 2016 |newspaper=Birmingham Mail |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831040913/http://www.birminghammail.co.uk/news/midlands-news/19th-anniversary-death-diana-princess-11821657 |url-status=live }}</ref>

By 10 September, the pile of flowers outside [[Kensington Gardens]] was {{convert|5|ft}} deep in places and the bottom layer had started to [[compost]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Jury |first=Louise |last2=Wolmar |first2=Christian |date=10 September 1997 |title=Safety fear sparks plea to stop flowers for Diana |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-independent-safety-fear-sparks-plea/199617752/ |work=The Independent |page=7 |via=Newspapers.com}}</ref> The people were quiet, queuing patiently to sign the book and leave their gifts.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml?html=/archive/1997/09/11/ndi411.html |title=Punch in face for teddy bear thief |last=Dutter |first=Barbie |date=11 September 1997 |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |access-date=8 June 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040913114236/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml?html=%2Farchive%2F1997%2F09%2F11%2Fndi411.html |archive-date=13 September 2004 |location=UK |url-status=dead |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Fresh flowers, teddy bears, and bottles of champagne were later donated and distributed among the sick, the elderly and children. Cards, personal messages and poems were collected and given to Diana's family.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/flowers.html |title=Diana Flowers to be Given to Needy |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=19 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220419183827/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/flowers.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Funeral and burial==
{{main|Funeral of Diana, Princess of Wales}}
{{further|Althorp#Diana grave, memorial, and exhibition}}
Early on, it was uncertain that Diana would receive a [[ceremonial funeral]], since she had lost the status of Her Royal Highness following her divorce from Prince Charles in 1996.<ref name=":5" />

Diana's death was met with extraordinary public expressions of grief,<ref>{{cite news |title=At the Palace Gates, Flowers and Tears—and Anger at the Press |last=Spolar |first=Christine |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=1 September 1997 |page=A27 }}</ref><ref name="WorldMourns">{{cite news |title=The World Grieves for Diana |last=Kampfner |first=John |newspaper=Financial Times |date=1 September 1997 |page=1 |quote=Her death triggered an outpouring of worldwide shock and grief perhaps not seen since the [[assassination of John F Kennedy]] in 1963... Within hours, grief was turning into anger for many. }}</ref> and her funeral at [[Westminster Abbey]] on 6 September drew an estimated 3&nbsp;million mourners and onlookers in London.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.londonnet.co.uk/ln/talk/news/diheadlines_previous1.html |title=Diana, Princess of Wales: The story so far |work=LondonNet |access-date=17 April 2006 |archive-date=3 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181003234235/http://www.londonnet.co.uk/ln/talk/news/diheadlines_previous1.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/september/6/newsid_2502000/2502307.stm |title=On this day 6&nbsp;September&nbsp;1997 |date=6 September 1997 |access-date=30 October 2007 |work=BBC News |archive-date=25 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210125020803/http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/september/6/newsid_2502000/2502307.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Outside the Abbey and in [[Hyde Park, London|Hyde Park]] crowds watched and listened to proceedings on large outdoor screens and speakers as guests filed in, including representatives of the many charities of which Diana was patron. Attendees included [[US First Lady]] [[Hillary Clinton]] and [[First Lady of France|French First Lady]] [[Bernadette Chirac]], as well as celebrities including Italian tenor [[Luciano Pavarotti]] and two friends of Diana, [[George Michael]] and [[Elton John]].<ref name="Time-Report"/><ref>Joal Ryan (6 September 1997) [http://uk.eonline.com/uberblog/b35134_farewell_mummy_princess_dianas_funeral.html "Farewell, 'Mummy': Princess Diana's Funeral"] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200315091634/http://uk.eonline.com/uberblog/b35134_farewell_mummy_princess_dianas_funeral.html |date=15 March 2020}}. [[E!]] online</ref> John performed a [[Candle in the Wind 1997|rewritten version]] of his song "[[Candle in the Wind]]" that was dedicated to her, known as "Goodbye England's Rose" or "Candle in the Wind 1997";<ref>''Elton's re-written song''{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/lyrics.html |title=Candle in the Wind |work=BBC News |access-date=22 October 2007 |archive-date=2 December 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101202160422/http://www.bbc.co.uk/politics97/diana/lyrics.html |url-status=live }}</ref> the single became the [[List of best-selling singles|best-selling single]] since UK and US singles charts began in the 1950s, with total sales exceeding 33&nbsp;million units.<ref>{{Cite magazine |title=Elton John |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/t/elton-john/ |access-date=14 May 2021 |magazine=Rolling Stone |archive-date=11 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511163735/https://www.rollingstone.com/t/elton-john/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Protocol (diplomacy)|Protocol]] was disregarded when the guests applauded the speech by Earl Spencer, who strongly criticised the press and indirectly criticised the Royal Family for their treatment of her.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/education/325163.stm |title='You brightened our lives' |date=21 April 1999 |work=BBC News |access-date=2 January 2010 |archive-date=1 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170901144103/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/education/325163.stm |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/1997/sep/08/diana |title=The funeral of Diana, Princess of Wales |work=The Guardian |first1=Kamal |last1=Ahmed |first2=Luke |last2=Harding |first3=Sarah |last3=Boseley |date=8 September 1997 |access-date=18 November 2023 |archive-date=19 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231119005116/https://www.theguardian.com/world/1997/sep/08/diana |url-status=live }}</ref> The funeral is estimated to have been watched by 31.5&nbsp;million viewers in Britain.<ref>{{cite news |title=Record 31.5m viewers switched on |last=Garner |first=Clare |newspaper=The Independent |date=8 September 1997 |page=3 }}</ref> Precise calculation of the worldwide audience is not possible, but it was estimated to be around 2.5&nbsp;billion.<ref name="TelevisionAudience">{{cite web |url=https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/some-2-5-billion-tv-viewers-watch-princess-dianas-funeral |title=Some 2.5 billion TV viewers watch Princess Diana's funeral |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=21 February 2019 |work=History |publisher=A&E Television Networks, LLC |access-date=24 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190524174445/https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/some-2-5-billion-tv-viewers-watch-princess-dianas-funeral |archive-date=24 May 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> The ceremony was broadcast in 44 languages.<ref name="brownbasil">{{cite journal |last1=Brown |first1=William J. |first2=Michael D. |last2=Basil |author3=Mihai C. Bocarnea |title=Social Influence of an International Celebrity: Responses to the Death of Princess Diana |journal=[[Journal of Communication]] |date=December 2003 |volume=53 |issue=4 |pages=587–605 |doi=10.1111/j.1460-2466.2003.tb02912.x }}</ref>

After the end of the ceremony, Diana's coffin was driven to [[Althorp]] in a [[Daimler Company|Daimler]] [[hearse]].<ref name="Hearse">{{cite news |first=Sholto |last=Byrnes |title=Pandora |newspaper=The Independent |date=1 May 2003 |page=17 }}</ref> Mourners cast flowers at the funeral procession for almost the entire length of its journey, and vehicles even stopped on the opposite carriageway of the [[M1 motorway]] as the cars passed.<ref>{{cite news |title=Farewell, Princess |last=Montalbano |first=William D. |newspaper=The Los Angeles Times |date=7 September 1997 |page=A1 |quote=Flowers tossed by mourners who lined streets for mile after mile lay heavily on the roof of the gleaming black hearse. As the vehicle approached the M-1 motorway, its driver had to stop to remove the bouquets that were obstructing his view. The procession formed the only northbound traffic on the motorway, and as it made its way toward Althorp, southbound vehicles were stopped as it passed. Drivers and passengers left their cars to watch, and many threw more flowers. }}</ref>

In a private ceremony, Diana was buried on an island in the middle of a lake called The Oval, which is part of the Pleasure Garden at Althorp.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/diana-the-last-resting-place-a-lake-island-grave-in-splendid-isolation-1237637.html |title=Diana – The Last Resting Place: A lake island grave in splendid isolation |date=5 September 1997 |newspaper=The Independent |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831044507/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/diana-the-last-resting-place-a-lake-island-grave-in-splendid-isolation-1237637.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The coffin bore a weight of a quarter of a tonne (250&nbsp;kg / approx. 550&nbsp;lb) as it was lined with lead,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Duncan |first=Amy |date=31 August 2018 |title=Why was Princess Diana's coffin lined with lead? |url=https://metro.co.uk/2018/08/31/why-was-diana-princess-of-wales-coffin-lined-with-lead-7900571/ |access-date=12 December 2022 |website=Metro |language=en |archive-date=12 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221212184618/https://metro.co.uk/2018/08/31/why-was-diana-princess-of-wales-coffin-lined-with-lead-7900571/ |url-status=live }}</ref> as is tradition with British royalty.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 September 2022 |title=Why does the coffin of Queen Elizabeth II have a lead lining? – Technology Org |url=https://www.technology.org/2022/09/11/why-does-the-coffin-of-queen-elizabeth-ii-have-lead-lining/ |access-date=12 December 2022 |website=technology.org |language=en-US |archive-date=23 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230923122006/https://www.technology.org/2022/09/11/why-does-the-coffin-of-queen-elizabeth-ii-have-lead-lining/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Her body was buried wearing a black [[Catherine Walker (fashion designer)|Catherine Walker]] dress and black tights, and holding a [[rosary]] in her hands. The rosary had been a gift from [[Mother Teresa]] of Calcutta, a confidante of Diana, who had died the day before her funeral. A visitors' centre is open during summer months, with an exhibition about Diana and a walk around the lake. All profits were donated to the [[Diana, Princess of Wales Memorial Fund]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.seattletimes.com/life/travel/memorials-to-the-peoples-princess-still-draw-admirers/ |title=Memorials to 'the people's princess' still draw admirers |last=Warner |first=Gary A. |date=31 August 2007 |work=[[The Seattle Times]] |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=23 November 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191123150914/https://www.seattletimes.com/life/travel/memorials-to-the-peoples-princess-still-draw-admirers/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Reactions==

===Royal family===
[[File:Correspondence regarding death of Diana, Princess of Wales (1997) (20393151714).jpg|thumb|Official correspondence by the [[New Zealand Government]] regarding Diana's death]]
Queen [[Elizabeth II]] expressed her dismay at Diana's death.<ref name=":11">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13466847/ |title=Queen 'Deeply Shocked, Distressed By Terrible News' |date=31 August 1997 |work=Honolulu-Star-Advertiser |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025253/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13466847/the-honolulu-advertiser/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Then-Prince Charles woke his sons before dawn to share the news.<ref name=":12">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467035/ |title=Charles Tells Boys Their Mother is Dead |date=31 August 1997 |work=Santa Cruz Sentinel |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025252/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467035/santa-cruz-sentinel/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Upon announcement of the death, the website of the Royal Family temporarily removed all its content and replaced it with a black background, displaying a picture of Diana accompanied by her name and dates of birth and death. An online book of condolence was also made available on the website for the public to post their personal tributes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/library/cyber/euro/090297euro.html |title=Mourning Diana: Online Chat Becomes a Book of Condolences |work=[[The New York Times]] |first=Bruno |last=Giussani |date=2 September 1997 |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417160910/https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/library/cyber/euro/090297euro.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> On Sunday morning after Diana's death, Queen Elizabeth II, Prince Charles, [[William, Prince of Wales|William]] and [[Prince Harry, Duke of Sussex|Harry]] all wore black to church services at [[Crathie Kirk]] near [[Balmoral Castle]].<ref name=":7" /> The royal family later issued a statement, saying Charles, William and Harry were "taking strength from" the public support and were "deeply touched" by and "enormously grateful" for it.<ref name="Guardian-Details">{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2017/aug/31/death-of-diana-the-week-that-rocked-britain |title=The death of Princess Diana: a week that rocked Britain |work=The Guardian |date=31 August 2017 |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=4 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220404130829/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2017/aug/31/death-of-diana-the-week-that-rocked-britain |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Deeply Touched">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mourn.html |title=Public Mourning Continues – Royal Family "Deeply Touched" |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417154038/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mourn.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Princes [[Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor|Andrew]] and [[Prince Edward, Duke of Edinburgh|Edward]] met the mourners outside Kensington Palace as a precautionary measure to test the public mood,<ref name="Guardian-Details"/> and Edward visited St James's Palace to sign the book of condolences.<ref name="Queen-Returns">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/royal.html |title=Queen to Address the Nation |access-date=18 April 2021 |archive-date=19 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190119121902/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/royal.html |url-status=live }}</ref> On their way from Crathie Kirk to Balmoral, Queen Elizabeth II, Prince Philip, Charles, William and Harry viewed the floral tributes and messages left by the public.<ref name="Guardian-Details"/><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/family.html |title=Solemn Royals Examine Balmoral Bouquets |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=15 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220815091243/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/family.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Charles and his sons returned to London on Friday, 5 September.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467545/ |title=Length of the Funeral Procession is Tripled |last=Leeman |first=Sue |date=4 September 1997 |work=The News Journal |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025253/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13467545/the-news-journal/ |url-status=live }}</ref> They made an unannounced visit to see the floral tributes left outside Kensington Palace.<ref name="Guardian-Details"/><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/princes.html |title=Tears as Princes Visit Palace |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210613200638/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/princes.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Queen Elizabeth II, who returned to London from Balmoral accompanied by Prince Philip, the [[Queen Elizabeth The Queen Mother|Queen Mother]], and [[Princess Margaret]], agreed to a television broadcast to the nation.<ref name="Streeter">{{Cite news |title=The Queen bows to her subjects |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/the-queen-bows-to-her-subjects-1237450.html |date=5 September 1997 |work=The Independent |access-date=27 February 2014 |location=London |first=Michael |last=Streeter |archive-date=7 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140307043556/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/the-queen-bows-to-her-subjects-1237450.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Queen-Returns"/> She viewed the floral tributes in front of Buckingham Palace and visited the [[Chapel Royal]] at St James's Palace, where Diana's body was [[Funeral#Visitation|remaining]], and met crowds that were in line to sign the books of condolence.<ref name="Guardian-Details"/><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/queen.html |title=Queen Broadcasts Live to Nation |work=BBC News |access-date=18 April 2021 |archive-date=27 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227044309/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/queen.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Diana's brother, Earl Spencer, and her former sister-in-law, [[Sarah, Duchess of York]], also visited St James's Palace.<ref name="Deeply Touched"/>

Queen Elizabeth II and the rest of the Royal Family were criticised for a rigid adherence to protocol, and their efforts to protect the privacy of Diana's grieving sons were interpreted as a lack of compassion.<ref name="BBC-flags">{{cite news |title=Flags at half mast for Diana |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/138289.stm |date=23 July 1998 |work=BBC News |quote=At the same time, the House of Windsor showed how stung it was in a statement issued by the Queen's press secretary Geoffrey Crawford. He said on television: 'The Royal Family have been hurt by suggestions that they are indifferent to the country's sorrow at the tragic death of the Princess of Wales.' |access-date=24 July 2017 |archive-date=29 July 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170729233907/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/138289.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> In particular, the refusal of Buckingham Palace to fly the [[Royal Standard (United Kingdom)|Royal Standard]] at [[half-mast]] provoked angry headlines in newspapers.<ref name="BBC-flags"/><ref>{{cite news |url=http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/04/diana.royals.under.fire/ |title=Royal family hurt by criticism over Diana |work=CNN |date=4 September 1997 |access-date=17 March 2018 |archive-date=20 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180320150943/http://edition.cnn.com/WORLD/9709/04/diana.royals.under.fire/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> The Palace's stance was one of royal protocol: no flag could fly over Buckingham Palace, as the Royal Standard is only flown when the monarch is in residence, and the Queen was then in Scotland. The Royal Standard never flies at half-mast as it is the Sovereign's flag and there is never an [[interregnum]] or vacancy in the monarchy, as the new monarch [[The king is dead, long live the king!|immediately succeeds]] his or her predecessor. Finally, as a compromise, the [[Union Flag]] was flown at half-mast as Queen Elizabeth II left for Westminster Abbey on the day of the funeral.<ref name="Streeter" /> This set a precedent, and [[Flags at Buckingham Palace|Buckingham Palace has subsequently flown the Union Flag]] when the monarch is not in residence.<ref>{{cite book |title=Constitutional and Administrative Law: Text with Materials |author=David Pollard, Neil |page=22 |publisher=Oxford University Press |year=2007 |isbn=978-0-199-28637-9 }}</ref>

A rift between Prince Charles and Queen Elizabeth II's private secretary, [[Sir Robert Fellowes]] (Diana's brother-in-law), was reported in the media over the nature of the funeral, with Charles demanding a public funeral and Fellowes supporting the Queen's idea of a private one.<ref name="Public-Private">{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1997/09/16/world/queen-issues-feisty-denial-of-disputes-over-funeral.html |title=Queen Issues Feisty Denial Of Disputes Over Funeral |work=The New York Times |first=Youssef M. |last=Ibrahim |date=16 September 1997 |access-date=17 March 2018 |archive-date=18 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180318055124/http://www.nytimes.com/1997/09/16/world/queen-issues-feisty-denial-of-disputes-over-funeral.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> The Palace later issued a statement denying such rumours.<ref name="Public-Private"/> Discussions were also held with the Spencer family and the British royal family as to whether Diana's [[HRH]] style needed to be restored posthumously, but Diana's family decided that it would be against Diana's wishes and no formal offer was made.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/hrh.html |title=Palace and Spencers Agree: No HRH |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=6 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210306091232/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/hrh.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The funeral committee at Buckingham Palace wanted William and Harry to have a bigger role in their mother's funeral and [[10 Downing Street|Downing Street]] officials suggested that they could walk in the funeral cortège,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://abcnews.go.com/GMA/News/princes-william-harry-walk-prince-philips-coffin-20/story?id=77084646 |title=Princes William, Harry walk together at Prince Philip's funeral |work=ABC News |first=Katie |last=Kindelan |date=17 April 2021 |access-date=27 August 2022 |archive-date=27 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220827170505/https://abcnews.go.com/GMA/News/princes-william-harry-walk-prince-philips-coffin-20/story?id=77084646 |url-status=live }}</ref> but faced opposition from Prince Philip, who reportedly stated "They've just lost their mother. You're talking about them as if they are commodities."<ref name="Guardian-Details"/> Prince Harry said in 2017 that the death of his mother caused [[severe depression]] and grief.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.latimes.com/entertainment/la-et-entertainment-news-updates-may-prince-william-talks-about-taboo-of-1496158945-htmlstory.html |title=Prince William talks about 'taboo' of mental illness and death of Diana in new interview |last=Hill |first=Libby |date=30 May 2017 |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |access-date=30 August 2017 |issn=0458-3035 |archive-date=30 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170830081715/http://www.latimes.com/entertainment/la-et-entertainment-news-updates-may-prince-william-talks-about-taboo-of-1496158945-htmlstory.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He later stated that what he experienced after his mother's death "was very much" [[post-traumatic stress disorder]] (PTSD).<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-harry-ptsi-diana-spare-b2258744.html |title=Prince Harry opens up about 'healing' from PTSI after Diana's death: What is Post Traumatic Stress Injury? |work=The Independent |first=Amber |last=Raiken |date=9 January 2023 |access-date=9 January 2023 |archive-date=9 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230109151909/https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-harry-ptsi-diana-spare-b2258744.html |url-status=live }}</ref> William was 15 and Harry was 12 when Diana died.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457935/ |title=Strong Bond With Mother May Help Young Princes Cope With Her Death |last=Vitez |first=Michael |date=1 September 1997 |work=Daily Record |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045225/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457935/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The boys received locks of their mother's hair from their aunt Lady Sarah McCorquodale once she returned from Paris according to Harry.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.etonline.com/prince-harrys-memoir-spare-past-romances-private-meetings-with-william-and-kate-and-more-royal |title=Prince Harry's Memoir 'Spare': Past Romances, Private Meetings With William and Kate and More Royal Bombshells |work=Entertainment Tonight |first=Rachel |last=McRady |date=10 January 2023 |access-date=10 January 2023 |archive-date=10 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230110201838/https://www.etonline.com/prince-harrys-memoir-spare-past-romances-private-meetings-with-william-and-kate-and-more-royal |url-status=live }}</ref>

Years later, William and Harry defended their father and grandmother's actions in the aftermath of their mother's death. Describing his father's role, Harry said: "[Our dad] was there for us — he was the one out of two left, and he tried to do his best and to make sure that we were protected and looked after."<ref name="People-Aug17">{{cite magazine |url=https://people.com/royals/prince-harry-says-his-father-prince-charles-did-his-best-after-dianas-death-he-was-there-for-us/ |title=Harry and William Open Up About the Moment They Learned of Diana's Death — as They Defend Their Father |magazine=People |first=Simon |last=Perry |date=22 August 2017 |access-date=17 December 2022 |archive-date=17 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221217070042/https://people.com/royals/prince-harry-says-his-father-prince-charles-did-his-best-after-dianas-death-he-was-there-for-us/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Speaking about his grandmother, William stated: "At the time, my grandmother wanted to protect her two grandsons and my father as well. Our grandmother deliberately removed the newspapers and things like that so there was nothing in the house to read."<ref name="People-Aug17"/> Diana's sister, Lady Sarah McCorquodale, also spoke in defence of the Queen's decision: "She did absolutely the right thing. If I'd been her, I'd have done that."<ref name="People-Aug17"/>

=== Politicians ===
[[File:President Clinton's Remarks on Death of Princess Diana (1997).webm|thumb|right|President Bill Clinton's remarks on Diana's death|215x215px]]
[[British prime minister]] [[Tony Blair]] said that he was "utterly devastated by the death of the Princess".<ref name=":5">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457830/ |title=Shocked Blair Salutes 'The People's Princess' |date=1 September 1997 |work=The Age |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045239/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457830/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/blairreact.html |title=Blair Pays Tribute To Diana |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=13 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210613075854/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/blairreact.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[US president]] [[Bill Clinton]] said that he and his wife, [[Hillary Clinton]], were "profoundly saddened" when they found out about her death.<ref name=":2" /> [[Kofi Annan]], the [[United Nations Secretary-General]] said that her death "has robbed the world of a consistent and committed voice for the improvement of the lives of suffering children worldwide".<ref name=":7">{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458272/ |title=Diana's Mourners Span the Globe |date=1 September 1997 |work=Honolulu Star-Advertiser |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045249/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458272/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In a telegram of condolences, [[German chancellor]] [[Helmut Kohl]] expressed the view that Diana had also become the victim of an "increasingly brutal and unscrupulous competition on the part of some of the media".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.welt.de/print-welt/article640694/England-trauert-um-Prinzessin-Diana.html |title=England trauert um Prinzessin Diana |date=1 September 1997 |access-date=13 November 2022 |work=Die Welt |language=German |archive-date=13 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113112422/https://www.welt.de/print-welt/article640694/England-trauert-um-Prinzessin-Diana.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In Australia, the Deputy Prime Minister, [[Tim Fischer]], condemned the paparazzi for their overzealous coverage of Diana.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13465810/ |title=Australian Leaders Condemn Intrusion Into Private Life |last=Middleton |first=Karen |date=2 September 1997 |work=The Age |access-date=31 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025254/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13465810/the-age/ |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Russian president]] [[Boris Yeltsin]] praised Diana's charity work in a statement saying, "All know of Princess Diana's big contribution to charitable work, and not only in Great Britain".<ref name="politics-react">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/worldreax.html |title=World Reaction to Diana's Death |work=BBC News |access-date=3 March 2018 |archive-date=22 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422220748/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/worldreax.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://people.com/royals/princess-diana-54th-birthday-commemoration-her-charities/moscow/ |title=Princess Diana and the Causes Close to Her Heart |magazine=People |first1=Cara Lynn |last1=Shultz |first2=Diana |last2=Pearl |date=23 June 2011 |access-date=3 March 2018 |archive-date=4 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180304172506/http://people.com/royals/princess-diana-54th-birthday-commemoration-her-charities/moscow/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Among other politicians who sent messages of condolences were Australian prime minister [[John Howard]], South African president [[Nelson Mandela]], Canadian prime minister [[Jean Chrétien]], New Zealand Prime Minister [[Jim Bolger]], and Israeli prime minister [[Benjamin Netanyahu]].<ref name="politics-react"/> The [[Australian House of Representatives]] and the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]] also passed parliamentary motions of condolence.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/search/display/display.w3p;query=Id%3A%22chamber%2Fhansardr%2F1997-09-01%2F0029%22 |title=CONDOLENCES;Diana, Princess of Wales – 01 Sep 1997 |work=Parliament of Australia |date=1 September 1997 |access-date=30 April 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1Iwfzv-Mt3CeE1ZSlJjVkVlVkk/view |title=First session, Forty-fifth parliament, 1996–97 |work=New Zealand Parliament |date=2 September 1997 |access-date=30 April 2021 |volume=563 |archive-date=30 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210430222817/https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B1Iwfzv-Mt3CeE1ZSlJjVkVlVkk/view |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Government of Canada]], as well as individual provinces in the country, set up online and in-person books of condolences in their parliament buildings and memorial services were held across the country.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1997/09/02/Canada-to-remember-Princess-Diana/9561873172800/ |title=Canada to remember Princess Diana |work=United Press International |date=2 September 1997 |access-date=30 April 2021 |archive-date=30 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210430223439/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1997/09/02/Canada-to-remember-Princess-Diana/9561873172800/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Following her death, delegates at an international conference in [[Oslo]] to ban landmines paid their tributes to Diana, who was an avid campaigner for banning the explosive devices.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mine.html |title=Championing the Anti-Land Mine Cause |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417175711/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mine.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Ottawa Treaty]], which created an international ban on the use of anti-personnel landmines, was adopted in Oslo, in September 1997 and signed by 122 states in [[Ottawa]] on 3 December 1997.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.mineaction.org/en/ihl-and-treaties |title=Convention on the Prohibition of the Use, Stockpiling, Production and Transfer of Anti-Personnel Mines and on Their Destruction |last=AP Mine Ban Convention Implementation Support Unit Geneva International Centre for Humanitarian Demining |access-date=16 September 2019 |archive-date=22 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201022174651/https://mineaction.org/en/ihl-and-treaties |url-status=live }}</ref> Diana's work on the landmines issue has been described as influential in the signing of the treaty.<ref>{{cite journal |url=http://www.icrc.org/web/eng/siteeng0.nsf/html/57JPJN |title=The background to the Ottawa process |last1=Maslen |first1=Stuart |last2=Herby |first2=Peter |date=31 December 1998 |journal=International Review of the Red Cross |issue=325 |pages=693–713 |doi=10.1017/S0020860400091579 |access-date=31 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080513115924/http://www.icrc.org/web/eng/siteeng0.nsf/html/57JPJN |archive-date=13 May 2008 |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

===Public===
In London, thousands of people carried bouquets and stood outside Buckingham Palace after the news of her death.<ref name=":7" /> People started bringing flowers within an hour after the news was shared.<ref name=":11" /> The BBC flew its flags at [[half-mast]].<ref name=":7" /> Both radio and television aired the British national anthem, "[[God Save the Queen]]", in response to Diana's death, as is precedent for the death of a member of the Royal Family.<ref name=":11" /> An [[elegy]] was published by [[Ted Hughes]] to mark her death.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://bbc.adactio.com/politics97/diana/poem.html |title=Poet Laureate Marks Diana's Funeral |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417225650/http://bbc.adactio.com/politics97/diana/poem.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Sporting events in the UK were rearranged, with demands for Scotland's Football Association chief executive to resign due to their delayed response to reschedule Scotland's World Cup qualifier.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/soccer.html |title=Calls For Farry to Quit Over Saturday Soccer Match |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=9 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210409230435/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/soccer.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

People in the US were shocked at her death.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457124/ |title=Shock Sets in Locally as News Spreads |last=Fasnacht |first=Don |date=31 August 1997 |work=Palladium-Item |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025255/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13457124/palladium-item/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In [[San Francisco]], around 14,000 people marched through the city in a procession on 5 September to pay tribute to Diana, honouring her for her work on behalf of AIDS patients.<ref name="BBC-Remember">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/world.html |title=World Remembers Diana |work=BBC News |access-date=1 May 2021 |archive-date=18 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210418102149/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/world.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In [[Los Angeles]], more than 2,500 people transformed a baseball field into a candle-lit altar in a memorial service prepared by an AIDS organisation.<ref name="BBC-Remember"/> In Paris, thousands of people visited the site of the crash and the hospital where Diana died, leaving bouquets, candles and messages.<ref name=":9">{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458747/ |title=Mourners in Paris Flock to the Scenes of Diana's Last Hours |last1=Swardson |first1=Anne |date=1 September 1997 |work=The Indianapolis Star |access-date=30 August 2017 |last2=Trueheart |first2=Charles |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045227/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458747/ |url-status=live }}</ref> People brought flowers and also attempted to visit the Hotel Ritz.<ref name=":9" /> On the eve of the funeral, 300 members of the British community in Paris took part in a service of commemoration.<ref name="BBC-Remember"/> Landmine victims in Angola and Bosnia also honoured Diana with separate services, pointing out how her efforts had helped raise awareness about the damage caused by landmines.<ref name="BBC-Remember"/> In Bosnia, a [[landmine]] survivor, Jasminko Bjelic, who had met Diana only three weeks earlier, said, "She was our friend."<ref name=":7" /> In Egypt, the homeland of Dodi Fayed, people visited the British embassy in Cairo to pay their tributes and sign a book of condolences.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/egypt.html |title=Dodi's Homeland Mourns Diana |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417205415/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/egypt.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Following her death many celebrities including actors and singers blamed the paparazzi and condemned their reckless behaviour.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mtv.html |title=Princess's Death Overshadows Music Awards |work=BBC News |access-date=3 March 2018 |archive-date=6 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181106003148/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/mtv.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13456430/ |title=Actor Decries Press Tactics |last=Bauder |first=Dave |date=31 August 1997 |work=Tallahassee Democrat |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045243/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13456430/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Mother Teresa]], who met Diana a few months before her death, expressed her sorrow and prayers were held at the [[Missionaries of Charity]] for Diana.<ref name="politics-react"/> The [[Bishop of Bradford (diocese)|Bishop of Bradford]] [[David Smith (bishop)|David Smith]] and the Bradford Council of Mosques held prayers by the Christian and Muslim communities.<ref name="Religions-United">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/rabbi.html |title=Religions United in Grief |work=BBC News |access-date=17 April 2021 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417204817/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/rabbi.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Jonathan Sacks]] led prayers by the Jewish community at the [[Western Marble Arch Synagogue]], and Cardinal [[Basil Hume]] presided over the Roman Catholic requiem mass held at [[Westminster Cathedral]].<ref name="Religions-United"/> Mother Teresa died on 5 September 1997, the day before Diana's funeral.<ref>{{Cite news |date=7 September 1997 |title=Good Night Sweet Princess |url=https://www.sundaytimes.lk/970907/news4.html |access-date=14 June 2026 |work=[[The Sunday Times (Sri Lanka)|The Sunday Times]]}}</ref>

==== Social and economic impact ====
During the four weeks following her funeral, the [[suicide rate]] in England and Wales rose by 17% and cases of [[deliberate self-harm]] by 44.3% compared with the average for that period in the four previous years. Researchers suggest that this was caused by the "[[identification (psychology)|identification]]" effect, as the greatest increase in suicides was by people most similar to Diana: women aged 25 to 44, whose suicide rate increased by over 45%.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Effect of death of Diana, princess of Wales on suicide and deliberate self-harm |last1=Hawton |first1=Keith |last2=Harriss |first2=Louise |last3=Simkin |first3=Sue |last4=Jusczcak |first4=Edmund |last5=Appleby |first5=Louise |last6=McDonnell |first6=Ros |last7=Amos |first7=Tim |last8=Kiernan |first8=Katy |last9=Parrott |first9=Hilary |journal=British Journal of Psychiatry |issue=5 |date=November 2000 |url=http://bjp.rcpsych.org/cgi/reprint/177/5/463 |access-date=4 June 2010 |pages=463–466 |pmid=11060002 |doi=10.1192/bjp.177.5.463 |volume=177 |doi-access=free |archive-date=18 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110618074444/http://bjp.rcpsych.org/cgi/reprint/177/5/463 |url-status=live }}</ref> Another research showed that 50% of Britons and 27% of Americans were deeply affected by her death as if someone they knew had died. It also concluded that in general women were more affected than men in both of the countries.<ref name="Gallup">{{cite news |url=http://news.gallup.com/vault/217355/gallup-vault-diana-death-personal.aspx |title=Gallup Vault: Diana's Death Was Personal for Many |work=Gallup |first=Lydia |last=Saad |date=30 August 2017 |access-date=22 February 2018 |archive-date=23 February 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180223051637/http://news.gallup.com/vault/217355/gallup-vault-diana-death-personal.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref> The same research showed that Diana's "charitable endeavors" and "ability to identify with ordinary people" were among the main factors that caused her to be admired and respected by the people.<ref name="Gallup"/> In the weeks after her death counselling services reported an increase in the number of phone calls by the people who were seeking help due to grief or distress.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/grief.html |title=A Nation's Loss |work=BBC News |access-date=3 March 2018 |archive-date=2 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181002091332/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/grief.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Diana's death mostly affected people who were already vulnerable and could identify with her as "a public figure perceived as psychologically troubled but who seemed to have made a constructive adjustment".<ref>{{cite journal |title=Death of Diana, Princess of Wales. People experiencing emotional difficulties react in different ways |last1=Williams |first1=Edwina R. L. |last2=Meadows |first2=Jean |last3=Catalàn |first3=Jose |journal=The BMJ |issue=7120 |date=29 November 1997 |pages=1467 |pmid=9418117 |volume=315 |doi=10.1136/bmj.315.7120.1467 |pmc=2127869 }}</ref> Another research described Diana's death and funeral as traumatic stressors with psychological impacts that could "be equated with traditional stressors identified in the trauma research literature".<ref>{{cite journal |title=Death of Diana, Princess of Wales. Her death and funeral rate as traumatic stressors. |last1=Shevlin |first1=Mark |last2=Brunsden |first2=Viv |last3=Walker |first3=Stephanie |last4=Davies |first4=Mark |last5=Ramkalawan |first5=Tina |journal=The BMJ |issue=7120 |date=29 November 1997 |pages=1467–1468 |pmid=9418118 |volume=315 |pmc=2127876 }}</ref> In the days after her funeral, an increase in the number of inappropriate hospital admissions was observed, whereas the number of admissions for traumatic injuries decreased for at least three months, showing a possible change in people's driving habits.<ref>{{cite journal |title=The 'Diana effect'. Hospital experienced a decrease in number of admissions for trauma |last1=Morgan-Jones |first1=Rhidian |last2=Smith |first2=Kevin |last3=Oakley |first3=Peter |journal=The BMJ |issue=7146 |date=6 June 1998 |pages=1750–1 |pmid=9614046 |doi=10.1136/bmj.316.7146.1750b |volume=316 |pmc=1113296 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |title=The 'Diana effect'. Number of inappropriate hospital admissions increased |last1=Berry |first1=Carolyn |last2=Mulholland |first2=Clive |last3=Stevenson |first3=Michael |last4=Reilly |first4=Philip |journal=The BMJ |issue=7146 |date=6 June 1998 |pages=1750 |pmid=9652938 |volume=316 |pmc=1113296 }}</ref> Her death was also associated with "30% reduction in calls to the police and a 28% drop in public order offences", yet despite its effect on increasing depression and traumatic stress, no significant increase was observed in the number of psychiatric emergencies in [[Edinburgh]].<ref>{{cite journal |title=The 'Diana effect'. There was no 'Diana effect' on numbers of psychiatric emergencies |last1=Mitchell |first1=John R. |journal=The BMJ |issue=7146 |date=6 June 1998 |pages=1750 |pmid=9652939 |volume=316 |pmc=1113296 }}</ref>

The national grieving for Diana had economic effects. In the short term, the [[Centre for Economics and Business Research]] (CEBR) estimated that retail sales dropped 1% that week. Traffic congestion in central London as crowds went to the palaces to pay homage likewise adversely affected [[productivity]], and the CEBR estimated that would cost businesses £200&nbsp;million, or a total loss of 0.1% of [[gross domestic product]] for the third quarter of 1997. However, in the long run the CEBR expected that to be offset by increased tourism and memorabilia sales.<ref name="Economic effect">{{cite news |last=Marks |first=Kathy |title=Diana 1961–1997: The legacy – Death to affect British economy |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/diana-1961-1997-the-legacy-death-to-affect-british-economy-1238058.html |newspaper=[[The Independent]] |date=7 September 1997 |access-date=24 October 2017 |archive-date=25 October 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171025132527/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/diana-1961-1997-the-legacy-death-to-affect-british-economy-1238058.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== Reception ====
Some criticised the reaction to Diana's death at the time as being "[[Hysteria|hysterical]]" and "irrational". As early as 1998, philosopher [[Anthony O'Hear]] identified the mourning as a defining point in the "sentimentalisation of Britain", a media-fuelled phenomenon where image and reality become blurred.<ref>[[O'Hear, Anthony]] (1998) "Diana, Queen of Hearts: Sentimentality personified and canonised" in Anderson and Mullen ''Faking It: The Sentimentalisation of Modern Society'', [[Social Affairs Unit]], {{ISBN|978-0-907631-75-0 }}</ref> [[Oasis (band)|Oasis]] bandleader [[Noel Gallagher]] responded to the reaction with, "The woman's dead. Shut up. Get over it".<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/1425553/oasis-noel-sounds-off-on-paul-mccartney-diana-mourners/ |title=Oasis' Noel Sounds Off On Paul McCartney, Diana Mourners |date=10 October 1997 |work=MTV |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=11 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190411172422/http://www.mtv.com/news/1425553/oasis-noel-sounds-off-on-paul-mccartney-diana-mourners/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> These criticisms were repeated on the tenth anniversary of the crash, when journalist [[Jonathan Freedland]] of ''[[The Guardian]]'' expressed the opinion that, "It has become an embarrassing memory, like a mawkish, self-pitying teenage entry in a diary&nbsp;... we cringe to think about it."<ref name="Fade-Legacy"/> In 2010, [[Theodore Dalrymple]] suggested "sentimentality, both spontaneous and generated by the exaggerated attention of the media, that was necessary to turn the death of the princess into an event of such magnitude thus served a political purpose, one that was inherently dishonest in a way that parallels the dishonesty that lies behind much sentimentality itself".<ref>{{cite book |title=Spoilt Rotten: The Toxic Cult of Sentimentality |last=Dalrymple |first=Theodore |publisher=Gibson Square Books Ltd |year=2010 |isbn=978-1-906142-61-2 |page=154 }}</ref>

The reactions following Diana's death were subject to criticism by [[Christopher Hitchens]]. His 1998 documentary ''Princess Diana: The Mourning After'' accused the British media of playing an essential role in creating a national, unchallengeable, and at times hysterical [[cult of personality]] surrounding Diana, whereas previously they had been extremely critical of her and the monarchy after she had separated and divorced from Charles, and was having an affair with Dodi Fayed. Hitchens claimed the public were behaving irrationally and that many appeared to not even know why they were mourning. He also scrutinised the level of censorship against criticism of Diana and the monarchy but was accused, in a review by ''[[The Independent]]'', of exaggerating on this point.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/television-its-a-state-of-total-dianarchy-1175026.html |title=Television: It's a state of total Dianarchy |author=Nicholas Barber |work=The Independent |date=30 August 1998 |access-date=21 April 2016 |archive-date=9 May 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160509030522/http://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/television-its-a-state-of-total-dianarchy-1175026.html |url-status=live }}</ref> ''[[Private Eye]]''{{'}}s sales dropped by one third after it ran a cover titled "Media to Blame", which attempted to criticise the instant switch in the media and the public's opinion of Diana after her death from critical to complimentary.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/voices/princess-diana-press-private-eye-censorship-paparazzi-public-grief-outpouring-a7922591.html |title=The attempts to censor Private Eye after Diana's death remind us of how a nation went mad with grief |newspaper=The Independent |first=Peyvand |last=Khorsandi |date=31 August 2017 |access-date=2 December 2020 |archive-date=11 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111173043/http://www.independent.co.uk/voices/princess-diana-press-private-eye-censorship-paparazzi-public-grief-outpouring-a7922591.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Hitchens's views were later supported by Jonathan Freedland of ''The Guardian'', who also questioned the reason behind the "outburst of mass hysteria" following Diana's death and described it as "an episode when the British public lost its characteristic cool and engaged in seven days of bogus sentimentality, whipped up by the media, and whose flimsiness was demonstrated when it vanished as quickly as it had appeared".<ref name="Fade-Legacy">{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2007/aug/13/britishidentity.monarchy |title=A moment of madness? |work=The Guardian |date=12 August 2007 |access-date=2 February 2017 |first=Jonathan |last=Freedland |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170203172846/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2007/aug/13/britishidentity.monarchy |archive-date=3 February 2017 }}</ref> Comparing Diana's funeral to [[Death and state funeral of Winston Churchill|that of Winston Churchill]], [[Peter Hitchens]] (brother of Christopher) observed the "difference in the self-discipline of the people and their attitudes" at the two historical events, with them being more restrained at Churchill's funeral but "un-English" at Diana's.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.c-span.org/video/?159943-1%2Fthe-abolition-britain |title=The Abolition of Britain |work=C-SPAN |date=20 October 2000 |access-date=1 December 2020 |archive-date=20 September 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200920172525/https://www.c-span.org/video/?159943-1%2Fthe-abolition-britain |url-status=live }}</ref>

Some cultural analysts disagreed. Sociologist [[Deborah Lynn Steinberg|Deborah Steinberg]] pointed out that many Britons associated Diana not with the Royal Family but with social change and a more liberal society: "I don't think it was hysteria, the loss of a public figure can be a touchstone for other issues."<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.ctv.ca/servlet/ArticleNews/story/CTVNews/20070830/diana_grieving_070831/20070831/ |title=Hysteria after Diana's death: A myth or reality? |author=Sandie Benitah |date=31 August 2007 |work=CTV News |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070903042643/http://www.ctv.ca/servlet/ArticleNews/story/CTVNews/20070830/diana_grieving_070831/20070831/ |archive-date=3 September 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Carol Wallace of ''[[People (magazine)|People]]'' magazine said that the fascination with Diana's death had to do with "the fairy tale failing to end happily – twice, first when she got divorced and now that she died".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458191/ |title=Fascination With Princess Diana As Great in Death as it Was in Life |last=Kane |first=Karen |date=1 September 1997 |work=Pittsburgh Post-Gazette |access-date=30 August 2017 |via=Newspapers.com |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045237/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/13458191/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Reflecting back on the event in the 2021 [[Apple TV+]] docuseries ''[[The Me You Can't See]]'', Diana's son Prince Harry said that he was surprised by the extent to which the public reacted to his mother's death. Referring to the day of her funeral, he said: "I'm just walking along and doing what was expected of me, showing the one-tenth of the emotion that everybody else was showing. This was my mum, you never even met her."<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://people.com/royals/prince-harry-on-sharing-grief-of-his-mothers-death-with-world-this-was-my-mum-you-never-even-met-her/ |title=Prince Harry on Sharing Grief of His Mother's Death with World: 'This Was My Mum, You Never Even Met Her' |magazine=People |first=Erin |last=Hill |date=20 May 2021 |access-date=27 May 2021 |archive-date=30 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210530234558/https://people.com/royals/prince-harry-on-sharing-grief-of-his-mothers-death-with-world-this-was-my-mum-you-never-even-met-her/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In his memoir ''[[Spare (memoir)|Spare]]'', he mentions meeting members of the public following his mother's death and "Hundreds and hundreds of hands that planted us again and again in front of our faces, with our fingers often wet. Of what? I wondered. Tears, I understood. I disliked the touch of those hands. What's more, I disliked how they made me feel guilty. Why were all of those people crying when I was neither crying nor able to cry?"<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/prince-harry-memoir-spare-leaked-prince-harry-says-he-disliked-meeting-people-after-mother-s-death-tears-i-101672935518025.html |title=Prince Harry says he 'disliked' meeting people after mother's death: 'Tears, I...' |work=Hindustan Times |date=5 January 2023 |access-date=5 January 2023 |archive-date=5 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105171401/https://www.hindustantimes.com/world-news/prince-harry-memoir-spare-leaked-prince-harry-says-he-disliked-meeting-people-after-mother-s-death-tears-i-101672935518025.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Memorials ==
{{further|Diana, Princess of Wales#Memorials}}
[[File:Flamme de la Liberté Place de l'Alma 2017-09-01 3.jpg|thumb|The ''[[Flame of Liberty]]'', the unofficial Diana memorial in Paris, France, the day after the 20th anniversary of her death. The ground is covered with flowers and other tributes, and the chain fence covered with [[love lock]]s.]]
In the years after Diana's death, many memorials were commissioned and dedicated to her. As a temporary memorial, the public co-opted the ''Flamme de la Liberté'' (''[[Flame of Liberty]]''), a monument near the Pont de l'Alma tunnel related to the French donation of the [[Statue of Liberty]] to the US. The messages of condolence have since been removed and its use as a Diana memorial has discontinued, though visitors still leave messages in her memory. A permanent memorial, the [[Diana, Princess of Wales Memorial Fountain]], was opened by the Queen in Hyde Park in London in 2004,<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.royalparks.org.uk/parks/hyde-park/hyde-park-attractions/diana-memorial-fountain |title=Diana Memorial Fountain |work=The Royal Parks |access-date=25 July 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130810143439/http://www.royalparks.org.uk/parks/hyde-park/hyde-park-attractions/diana-memorial-fountain |archive-date=10 August 2013 }}</ref> followed by [[Statue of Diana, Princess of Wales|a statue]] in the Sunken Garden of Kensington Palace, which was unveiled by her sons on her 60th birthday in 2021.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-57684597 |title=William and Harry unite to unveil Diana statue at Kensington Palace |work=BBC News |first=Hazel |last=Shearing |date=1 July 2021 |access-date=1 July 2021 |archive-date=1 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210701135505/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-57684597 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Following her death, a member of the [[Millennium Dome]]'s board suggested the project be refashioned and extended "to accommodate, for example, a hospital, businesses, charities, private residences, and the whole thing named 'the Princess Diana Centre{{'"}}. The idea was later scrapped.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.sky.com/story/bill-clinton-turned-down-tea-with-the-queen-five-things-we-learned-from-newly-declassified-papers-12359777 |title=Bill Clinton turned down tea with the Queen: Five things we learned from newly declassified papers |work=Sky News |first=Connor |last=Sephton |date=20 July 2021 |access-date=20 July 2021 |archive-date=20 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210720130500/http://news.sky.com/story/bill-clinton-turned-down-tea-with-the-queen-five-things-we-learned-from-newly-declassified-papers-12359777 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Inquests==
{{Further|Operation Paget}}
[[File:Henri PAUL.png|thumb|Henri Paul, the driver involved in the crash, in 1988]]
Under [[English law]], an [[Inquest (England and Wales)|inquest]] is required in cases of sudden or unexplained death.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.direct.gov.uk/en/Governmentcitizensandrights/Death/WhatToDoAfterADeath/DG_066713 |title=Coroners, post-mortems and inquests |work=[[Directgov]] |access-date=8 June 2010 |archive-date=22 December 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101222122830/http://www.direct.gov.uk/en/Governmentcitizensandrights/Death/WhatToDoAfterADeath/DG_066713 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":0">{{cite news |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/style/2004/10/princess-diana-death-investigations |title=The Diana Mysteries |last=Sancton |first=Tom |date=1 October 2004 |work=Vanities |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=14 June 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170614083920/http://www.vanityfair.com/style/2004/10/princess-diana-death-investigations |url-status=live }}</ref> A French judicial investigation had already been carried out, but the 6,000-page report was never published.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3190301.stm |title=Diana and Dodi inquests announced |work=BBC News |date=29 August 2003 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=12 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612114654/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3190301.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> On 6 January 2004, almost seven years after Diana's death, an inquest into the crash opened in London held by [[Michael Burgess (coroner)|Michael Burgess]], the [[coroner of the Queen's Household]].<ref name=":0" /> The coroner asked the Metropolitan Police commissioner, Sir [[John Stevens, Baron Stevens of Kirkwhelpington|John Stevens]], to make inquiries in response to speculation that the deaths were not an accident.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3371053.stm |title=Diana crash investigation ordered |work=BBC News |date=6 January 2004 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=12 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612115000/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3371053.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Forensic scientist [[Angela Gallop]] was commissioned to examine the forensic evidence.<ref name="Imogen">{{cite news |last=West-Knights |first=Imogen |url=https://www.theguardian.com/science/2022/mar/24/queen-of-crime-solving-angela-gallop-forensic-science |title=The queen of crime-solving |work=The Guardian |date=24 March 2022 |access-date=25 March 2022 |archive-date=24 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220324222145/https://www.theguardian.com/science/2022/mar/24/queen-of-crime-solving-angela-gallop-forensic-science |url-status=live }}</ref> The police investigation reported its findings in [[Operation Paget]] in December 2006.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1544282/Lord-Stevens-report-Claims-and-conclusions.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220111/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1544282/Lord-Stevens-report-Claims-and-conclusions.html |archive-date=11 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Lord Stevens report: Claims and conclusions |work=The Daily Telegraph |access-date=30 August 2017}}{{cbignore }}</ref>

In January 2006, Lord Stevens explained in an interview on ''[[GMTV]]'' that the case is substantially more complex than once thought. ''[[The Sunday Times]]'' wrote on 29 January 2006 that agents of the British secret service were [[cross-examined]] because they were in Paris at the time of the crash. It was suggested that these agents might have exchanged the blood test from Henri Paul with another blood sample (although no evidence for this has been forthcoming).<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/article/0,,2087-2014816,00.html |title=Doubts cast over blood samples in Diana inquiry |last=Leppard |first=David |date=29 January 2006 |newspaper=[[The Sunday Times]] |access-date=1 May 2010 |location=London |archive-date=26 July 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080726222558/http://www.timesonline.co.uk/article/0,,2087-2014816,00.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><!--Westdeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung|date=30 January 2006|title=Auftrieb für Spekulationen (in: Aus Aller Welt)}} {{de icon }}</ref> --><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.theroyalist.net/content/view/372/2/ |title=The Diana Investigation: What Lord Stevens Really Said |date=30 January 2006 |work=The Royalist |first=Joanne |last=Leyland |access-date=19 June 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060705204028/http://www.theroyalist.net/content/view/372/2/ |archive-date=5 July 2006 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The inquests into the deaths of Diana and Fayed opened on 8 January 2007, with Dame [[Elizabeth Butler-Sloss]] acting as Deputy Coroner of the Queen's Household for the Diana inquest and Assistant Deputy Coroner for Surrey in relation to the Fayed inquest. Butler-Sloss originally intended to sit without a jury;<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/article1293183.ece |title=No jury for Diana inquest |author=Lee Glendinning |newspaper=[[The Times]] |date=15 January 2007 |access-date=4 June 2010 |location=London |archive-date=31 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220831025257/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/ |url-status=dead |url-access=subscription }}</ref> this decision was later overturned by the [[High Court of Justice]],<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/6411091.stm |title=Diana inquest to be heard by jury |work=BBC News |date=2 March 2007 |access-date=4 June 2010 |archive-date=5 October 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071005151504/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/6411091.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> as well as the jurisdiction of the coroner of the Queen's Household. On 24 April 2007, Butler-Sloss stepped down, saying she lacked the experience required to deal with an inquest with a jury.<ref name="inquests">{{cite web |url=http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/faq/index.htm |title=Inquests into the deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed: FAQs |year=2008 |work=Coroner's Inquests into the Deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed |publisher=Judicial Communications Office |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20080521144222/http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/faq/index.htm |archive-date=21 May 2008 |url-status=dead |access-date=4 June 2010 }}</ref> The role of coroner for the inquests was transferred to [[Lord Justice Scott Baker]], who formally took up the role on 13 June as Coroner for Inner West London.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2008/mar/31/diana.timeline |title=Timeline: The Diana inquiries |newspaper=The Guardian |date=31 March 2008 |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045625/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2008/mar/31/diana.timeline |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 27 July 2007, Baker, following representations for the lawyers of the interested parties, issued a list of issues likely to be raised at the inquest, many of which had been dealt with in great detail by Operation Paget:

{{blockquote
|# Whether driver error on the part of Henri Paul caused or contributed to the cause of the collision
# Whether Henri Paul's ability to drive was impaired through drink or drugs
# Whether a Fiat Uno or any other vehicle caused or contributed to the collision
# Whether the actions of the Paparazzi caused or contributed to the cause of the collision
# Whether the road/tunnel layout and construction were inherently dangerous and, if so, whether this contributed to the collision
# Whether any bright/flashing lights contributed to or caused the collision and, if so, their source
# Whose decision it was that Diana and Dodi Al Fayed should leave from the rear entrance to the Ritz and that Henri Paul should drive the vehicle
# Henri Paul's movements between 7 and 10&nbsp;pm on 30 August 1997
# The explanation for the money in Henri Paul's possession on 30 August 1997 and in his bank account
# Whether Andanson, a photographer who followed the princess in the week before her death, was in Paris on the night of the collision
# Whether Diana's life would have been saved if she had reached hospital sooner or if her medical treatment had been different
# Whether Diana was pregnant
# Whether Diana and Dodi Al Fayed were about to announce their engagement
# Whether and, if so in what circumstances, the Princess of Wales feared for her life
# The circumstances relating to the purchase of the ring
# The circumstances in which Diana's body was embalmed
# Whether the evidence of [[Richard Tomlinson|Tomlinson]] throws any light on the collision
# Whether the British or any other security services had any involvement in the collision
# Whether there was anything sinister about (i) the Cherruault burglary{{clarify|date=October 2022}} or (ii) the disturbance at the Big Pictures agency
# Whether correspondence belonging to Diana (including some from Prince Philip) has disappeared, and if so the circumstances.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/directions_decs/issueslist_27072007.htm |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20080521144222/http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/directions_decs/issueslist_27072007.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 May 2008 |title=Inquests into the deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed: Pre-inquest Hearing – 27&nbsp;July 2007 – List of Likely Issues |access-date=4 June 2010 |work=Coroner's Inquests into the Deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed |publisher=Judicial Communications Office |date=27 July 2007 }}</ref>}}

The inquests officially began on 2 October 2007 with the swearing of a jury of six women and five men. Lord Justice Baker delivered a lengthy [[opening statement]] giving general instructions to the jury and introducing the evidence.<ref>{{cite web |title=Inquests into the deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed: Hearing transcript – 2&nbsp;October 2007 Morning |url=http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/021007am.htm |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20080521144222/http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/021007am.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 May 2008 |work=Coroner's Inquests into the Deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed |publisher=Judicial Communications Office |access-date=4 June 2010 |year=2008 }}</ref> The BBC reported that Mohamed Al-Fayed, having earlier reiterated his claim that his son and Diana were murdered by the Royal Family, immediately criticised the opening statement as biased.<ref>{{cite news |title=BBC Report of First Day of Inquests, see video report with Nicholas Witchell |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/7023227.stm |date=2 October 2007 |access-date=2 January 2010 |work=BBC News |archive-date=15 October 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071015214332/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/7023227.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

The inquest heard evidence from people connected with Diana and the events leading to her death, including Rees-Jones, Mohamed Al-Fayed, [[Paul Burrell]], Diana's stepmother, and the former head of [[MI6]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Al Fayed gets his 'moment' in court |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/7250241.stm |work=BBC News |date=18 February 2008 |access-date=2 January 2010 |first=Caroline |last=McClatchey |archive-date=3 February 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090203111944/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7250241.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

{{Wikinews|Princess Diana jury returns verdict of "unlawful killing"}}
Lord Justice Baker began his summing up to the jury on 31 March 2008.<ref name="autogenerated2">{{cite news |title=Duke 'did not order death of Diana' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/7322204.stm |work=BBC News |date=31 March 2008 |access-date=2 January 2010 |archive-date=6 April 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080406015159/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/7322204.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> He opened by telling the jury "no-one except you and I and, I think, the gentleman in the public gallery with Diana and Fayed painted on his forehead sat through every word of evidence" and concluded that there was "not a shred of evidence" that Diana's death had been ordered by [[Prince Philip]] or organised by the security services.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hearing transcripts: 2&nbsp;April&nbsp;2008 Morning session |date=31 March 2008 |url=http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/310308am.htm |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20080521144222/http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/310308am.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 May 2008 |work=Coroner's Inquests into the Deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed |publisher=Judicial Communications Office |access-date=28 May 2009 }}</ref> He concluded his summing up on Wednesday, 2 April 2008.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hearing transcripts: 4&nbsp;April&nbsp;2008 Morning session |date=2 April 2008 |url=http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/020408am.htm |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20080521144222/http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/020408am.htm |url-status=dead |archive-date=21 May 2008 |work=Coroner's Inquests into the Deaths of Diana, Princess of Wales and Mr Dodi Al Fayed |publisher=Judicial Communications Office |access-date=28 May 2009 }}</ref> After summing up, the jury retired to consider five verdicts, namely [[unlawful killing]] by the negligence of either or both the following vehicles or Paul; accidental death or an [[open verdict]].<ref name="autogenerated2" /> The jury decided on 7 April 2008 that Diana had been unlawfully killed by the "grossly negligent driving of the following vehicles [the paparazzi] and of the Mercedes driver Henri Paul".<ref name="verdict">{{cite web |url=http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/verdict.htm |title=Hearing transcripts: 7&nbsp;April 2008 – Verdict of the jury |publisher=Judicial Communications Office |archive-url=http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20080521144222/http://www.scottbaker-inquests.gov.uk/hearing_transcripts/verdict.htm |archive-date=21 May 2008 |url-status=dead |access-date=15 August 2010 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Princess Diana unlawfully killed |date=7 April 2008 |work=BBC News |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7328754.stm |access-date=5 August 2008 |archive-date=28 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170828152624/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7328754.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Princes William and Harry released a statement in which they said that they "agree with their verdicts and are both hugely grateful".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7335872.stm |title=Princes welcome inquest verdicts |work=BBC News |date=8 April 2008 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=25 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180525071720/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7335872.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Mohamed Al-Fayed also said that he would accept the verdict and "abandon his 10-year campaign to prove that Diana and Dodi were murdered in a conspiracy".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7337789.stm |title=Al Fayed abandons Diana campaign |work=BBC News |date=8 April 2008 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=6 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120306004036/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7337789.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

The cost of the inquiry exceeded £12.5&nbsp;million, the coroner's inquest cost £4.5&nbsp;million; a further £8&nbsp;million was spent on the Metropolitan Police investigation. It lasted 6 months and heard 250 witnesses, with the cost heavily criticised in the media.<ref>{{cite news |title=Diana inquiry costs exceed £12m |date=15 April 2008 |work=BBC News |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7349059.stm |access-date=5 August 2008 |archive-date=24 August 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130824042520/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7349059.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

In his memoir, Diana's younger son Prince Harry mentions how the summary conclusion of investigations into his mother's death was "simplistic and absurd".<ref name="Sky-Synopsis">{{cite news |url=https://news.sky.com/story/prince-harry-asked-his-father-not-to-marry-camilla-as-moment-he-was-told-about-his-mothers-car-accident-revealed-in-book-12780602 |title=Harry opens up about how he learned of Diana's car crash – and says King barred Meghan from Balmoral after Queen's death |work=Sky News |date=5 January 2023 |access-date=5 January 2023 |archive-date=5 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105171116/https://news.sky.com/story/prince-harry-asked-his-father-not-to-marry-camilla-as-moment-he-was-told-about-his-mothers-car-accident-revealed-in-book-12780602 |url-status=live }}</ref> He writes that even if the driver "had been drunk, he wouldn't have had any problem driving through such a short tunnel."<ref name="Sky-Synopsis"/> He questions why the paparazzi that had been following her and the people who sent them were not in prison, unless it was all due to "corruption and cover-ups being the order of the day?"<ref name="Sky-Synopsis"/> Harry claims that he and his brother had been planning on issuing a statement to ask jointly for the investigation to be reopened but "those who decided dissuaded us".<ref name="Sky-Synopsis"/>

== Related lawsuits ==
Nine photographers, who had been following Diana and Dodi in 1997, were charged with [[manslaughter]] in France.<ref name=":1">{{cite news |url=http://mobile-feeds.cbsnews.com/news/diana-crash-photogs-acquitted/ |title=Diana Crash Photogs Acquitted |last=Cosgrove-Mather |first=Bootie |date=28 November 2003 |work=CBS News |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831043832/http://mobile-feeds.cbsnews.com/news/diana-crash-photogs-acquitted/ |url-status=live }}</ref> France's "highest court" dropped the charges in 2002.<ref name=":1" />

Three photographers who took pictures of the aftermath of the crash on 31 August 1997 had their photographs confiscated and were tried for [[invasion of privacy]] for taking pictures through the open door of the crashed car.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/4442659.stm |title=Diana paparazzi trio face retrial |work=BBC News |date=13 April 2005 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=29 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180529015026/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/4442659.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> The photographers, who were part of the "paparazzi", were acquitted in 2003.<ref name=":1" /> However, following several appeals and a partial retrial, they were convicted of invasion of privacy in 2006 and sentenced to pay a symbolic fine of one euro each, along with the costs of announcing their convictions in three publications.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2006/feb/22/pressandpublishing.france |title=Diana crash photographers fined |work=The Guardian |date=22 February 2006 |access-date=16 November 2023 |archive-date=16 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231116174111/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2006/feb/22/pressandpublishing.france |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Conspiracy theories==
{{main|Conspiracy theories about the death of Diana, Princess of Wales}}

Although the initial French investigation found that Diana had died as a result of an accident, several [[Death of Diana, Princess of Wales conspiracy theories|conspiracy theories]] have been raised.<ref name="theories">{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/magazine/3372593.stm |title=The theory about conspiracies |work=BBC News |date=6 January 2004 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=12 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180612113908/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/magazine/3372593.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> Since February 1998, Fayed's father, Mohamed Al-Fayed, has claimed that the crash was a result of a conspiracy,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/55800.stm |title=Diana crash was a conspiracy – Al Fayed |date=12 February 1998 |access-date=5 August 2008 |work=BBC News |archive-date=14 December 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081214212517/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/55800.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> and later contended that the crash was orchestrated by [[MI6]] on the instructions of the Royal Family.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7251568.stm |title=Point-by-point: Al Fayed's claims |date=19 February 2008 |work=BBC News |access-date=5 August 2008 |archive-date=12 January 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090112201131/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7251568.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> His claims were dismissed by a French judicial investigation<ref name="autogenerated1"/> and by Operation Paget.<ref>{{cite news |title=Diana death a 'tragic accident' |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/6179275.stm |date=14 December 2006 |work=BBC News |access-date=5 August 2008 |archive-date=26 June 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080626220037/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/6179275.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> On 7 April 2008, Lord Justice Baker's inquest into the deaths of Diana and Fayed ended with the jury concluding that they were the victims of an "unlawful killing" by Henri Paul and the drivers of the following vehicles.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584160/Diana-jury-blames-paparazzi-and-Henri-Paul-for-her-unlawful-killing.html |title=Diana jury blames paparazzi and Henri Paul for her 'unlawful killing' |last=Rayner |first=Gordon |date=7 April 2008 |work=The Daily Telegraph |access-date=12 October 2013 |location=London |archive-date=25 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180425124749/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1584160/Diana-jury-blames-paparazzi-and-Henri-Paul-for-her-unlawful-killing.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Additional factors were "the impairment of the judgment of the driver of the Mercedes through alcohol" and "the death of the deceased was caused or contributed to by the fact that the deceased was not wearing a seat belt, the fact that the Mercedes struck the pillar in the Alma Tunnel rather than colliding with something else".<ref name="verdict" />

On 17 August 2013, [[Scotland Yard]] revealed that they were examining the credibility of information from a source that alleged that Diana was murdered by a member of the British military.<ref name="reu17">{{cite news |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-britain-diana/british-police-studying-new-information-on-princess-diana-death-idUSBRE97G09H20130817 |title=British police studying new information on Princess Diana death |last=Addison |first=Stephen |date=17 August 2013 |newspaper=Reuters |access-date=18 August 2013 |location=London |archive-date=22 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180922123953/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-britain-diana/british-police-studying-new-information-on-princess-diana-death-idUSBRE97G09H20130817 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2013/08/17/world/europe/england-princess-diana/index.html |title=Newly revealed conspiracy claim in Princess Diana death sparks talk |author=Michael Pearson |date=17 August 2013 |newspaper=CNN |access-date=18 August 2013 |author2=Atika Shubert |archive-date=18 August 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130818140425/http://edition.cnn.com/2013/08/17/world/europe/england-princess-diana/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==In the media==
[[File:After Dark special edition on 13 September 1997.jpg|thumb|A special ''[[After Dark (TV programme)|After Dark]]'' television discussion – ''After Diana'' – broadcast on [[Channel 4]] on 13 September 1997]]
Actor [[George Clooney]] was critical of several tabloids and paparazzi agencies following Diana's death. A few of the tabloids [[boycott]]ed Clooney following the outburst, stating that he "owed a fair portion of his celebrity" to the tabloids and photo agencies in question.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,1101970915-138281,00.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070111095627/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,1101970915-138281,00.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=11 January 2007 |title=Hey, wanna buy some pix? |author-link=Richard Zoglin |author=Richard Zoglin |date=24 June 2001 |magazine=Time |access-date=22 October 2007 }}</ref> In September 1997, a number of supermarket chains in the US removed the 9 September issue of the ''[[National Enquirer]]'', which bore the title "Di Goes Sex Mad".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1997/09/03/stores-withdraw-tabloid-editions-with-inappropriate-diana-stories/31f215f4-26c9-4fc0-a150-e4da7e554f53/ |title=Stores Withdraw Tabloid Editions with 'Inappropriate' Diana Stories |newspaper=The Washington Post |first=Cindy |last=Loose |date=3 September 1997 |access-date=1 September 2022 |archive-date=1 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901161525/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/politics/1997/09/03/stores-withdraw-tabloid-editions-with-inappropriate-diana-stories/31f215f4-26c9-4fc0-a150-e4da7e554f53/ |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> The paper's editor described the incident as "an unfortunate circumstance", which was not meant to coincide with her death.<ref name="Independent-tabloids">{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/media/american-editors-rush-to-keep-it-all-tasteful-1237339.html |title=American editors rush to keep it all tasteful |work=The Independent |first=Mary |last=Dejevsky |date=3 September 1997 |access-date=31 August 2022 |archive-date=1 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220901084432/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/media/american-editors-rush-to-keep-it-all-tasteful-1237339.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Another American tabloid, ''[[Globe (tabloid)|Globe]]'', issued an apology for its headline "To Di For".<ref name="Independent-tabloids"/>

Diana was ranked third in the 2002 ''[[100 Greatest Britons|Great Britons]]'' poll sponsored by the BBC and voted for by the British public, after [[Sir Winston Churchill]] (1st) (a distant cousin), and [[Isambard Kingdom Brunel]] (2nd), just above [[Charles Darwin]] (4th), [[William Shakespeare]] (5th), and [[Isaac Newton]] (6th). That same year, another British poll named Diana's death as the most important event in the country's last 100 years.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.upi.com/Business_News/Security-Industry/2002/08/27/Poll-queries-British-sense-of-history/UPI-69441030476098/ |title=Poll queries British sense of history |last=Almond |first=Peter |date=27 August 2002 |work=United Press International |location=Washington, D.C. |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160115144621/http://www.upi.com/Business_News/Security-Industry/2002/08/27/Poll-queries-British-sense-of-history/UPI-69441030476098/ |archive-date=15 January 2016 |url-status=live |access-date=28 July 2012 }}</ref> Historian Nick Barrett criticised this outcome as being "a pretty shocking result".<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2002/aug/31/20020831-041721-1985r/ |title=Rue Britannia: Diana's death tops poll |date=31 August 2002 |newspaper=[[The Washington Times]] |access-date=28 July 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160115144622/http://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2002/aug/31/20020831-041721-1985r/ |archive-date=15 January 2016 |url-status=live |location=Washington, D.C. }}</ref>

Later in 2004, the [[CBS]] programme ''[[48 Hours (TV program)|48 Hours]]'' broadcast photos from the crash scene which were "part of a 4,000-page French government report". They showed an intact rear side and centre section of the Mercedes, including one of an unbloodied Diana with no outward injuries crouched on the rear floor with her back to the right passenger seat{{snd}}the right rear door is fully open. The release of these pictures was poorly received in the UK, where it was felt that the privacy of Diana was being infringed.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2004/apr/22/pressandpublishing.broadcasting |title=Spencer 'sickened' by Diana pictures |date=22 April 2004 |newspaper=The Guardian |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831045915/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2004/apr/22/pressandpublishing.broadcasting |url-status=live }}</ref> Buckingham Palace, Prime Minister Tony Blair and Diana's brother condemned the action, while CBS defended its decision saying that the pictures "are placed in journalistic context – an examination of the medical treatment given to Princess Diana just after the crash".<ref>{{cite news |title=Family's shock over Diana images |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3647849.stm |work=BBC News |date=22 April 2004 |access-date=29 May 2018 |archive-date=30 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180530042123/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/3647849.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 13 July 2006, Italian magazine ''[[Chi (magazine)|Chi]]'' published photographs that showed Diana amid the wreckage of the car crash;<ref name="photo1">{{cite news |url=http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/07/14/world/main1805875.shtml |title=Photos Of Dying Diana Outrage Britain, Italian Magazine Printed Photos Of Princess At Crash Site In 1997 |work=CBS News |date=14 July 2006 |access-date=13 October 2008 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081017100235/http://www.cbsnews.com/stories/2006/07/14/world/main1805875.shtml |archive-date=17 October 2008 }}</ref> the photos were released despite an unofficial blackout on such photographs being published.<ref name="photo2">{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/5181226.stm |title=Princes' 'sadness' at Diana photo |work=BBC News |date=14 July 2006 |access-date=13 October 2008 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071018063600/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/5181226.stm |archive-date=18 October 2007 }}</ref>{{efn|The photographs, taken minutes after the crash, show her slumped in the back seat while a paramedic attempts to fit an oxygen mask over her face.}} The editor of ''Chi'' defended his decision by saying he published the photographs simply because they had not been previously seen, and he felt the images were not disrespectful to the memory of Diana.<ref name="photo2" />

British newspaper the ''[[Daily Express]]'' was criticised for continued and sustained coverage of Diana following her death. A 2006 report in ''The Guardian'' showed that the newspaper had mentioned her in numerous recent news stories, with headlines including, "Perhaps Diana should have worn seatbelt", "Diana inquiry chief's laptop secrets stolen", "£250,000 a year bill to run Diana fountain" and "Diana seatbelt sabotage probe".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2006/may/09/pressandpublishing.themonarchy |title=Any old Diana headline here |date=9 May 2006 |newspaper=The Guardian |access-date=30 August 2017 |archive-date=31 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831002447/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2006/may/09/pressandpublishing.themonarchy |url-status=live }}</ref>

The events from Diana's death to her funeral became the subject of a 2006 film, ''[[The Queen (2006 film)|The Queen]]'', with [[Helen Mirren]] in the title role.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Bastin |first1=Giselle |title=Filming the Ineffable: Biopics of the British Royal Family |journal=A/B: Auto/Biography Studies |date=January 2009 |volume=24 |issue=1 |pages=34–52 |doi=10.1080/08989575.2009.10846787 |s2cid=192035932 |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/article/394170 |access-date=25 November 2023 |archive-date=3 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303233616/http://muse.jhu.edu/journals/abs/summary/v024/24.1.bastin.html |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref> The eight weeks leading up to her death and funeral are dramatised in the first four episodes of season six of the [[Netflix]] series, ''[[The Crown (TV series)|The Crown]]'', in which Diana is portrayed by [[Elizabeth Debicki]].<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://people.com/what-happens-to-princess-diana-in-crown-season-6-8404309 |title=What Happens to Princess Diana in The Crown Season 6? A Look at the Real-Life Events |magazine=People |first=Stephanie |last=Kaloi |date=17 November 2023 |access-date=18 November 2023 |archive-date=17 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231117232121/https://people.com/what-happens-to-princess-diana-in-crown-season-6-8404309 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Internet coverage===
Diana's death occurred at a time when [[Internet]] use in the developed world was booming, and several national newspapers and at least one British regional newspaper had already launched online news services. [[BBC News]] had set up online coverage of the [[1997 United Kingdom general election|general election]] earlier in 1997<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.freshnetworks.com/blog/2008/06/major-events-influenced-bbcs-news-online/ |title=Major events influenced BBC's news online |date=5 June 2008 |work=Fresh Networks |access-date=30 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101228193301/http://www.freshnetworks.com/blog/2008/06/major-events-influenced-bbcs-news-online/ |archive-date=28 December 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref> and as a result of the widespread public and media attention surrounding Diana's death, BBC News swiftly created a website featuring news coverage of Diana's death and the events that followed it. Diana's death helped BBC News officials realise how important online news services were becoming, and a full [[BBC News Online|online news service]] was launched on 4 November that year, alongside the launch of the BBC's rolling news channel [[BBC News 24]] on 9 November.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/special/politics97/diana/ |title=Diana Remembered |work=BBC News |access-date=29 May 2013 |archive-date=24 January 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110124204525/http://www.bbc.co.uk/politics97/diana/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Television ===
In the United Kingdom, the [[BBC]] were the first to announce the crash, with [[Martyn Lewis]] on the air on both [[BBC1]] (interrupting the film ''[[Borsalino (film)|Borsalino]]'') and [[BBC2]]. Lewis would later announce Diana's death at 6{{nbsp}}am.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Newsreader Martyn Lewis recalls the day he broke the news of Princess Diana's death |url=https://www.radiotimes.com/tv/current-affairs/newreader-martyn-lewis-recalls-the-day-he-broke-the-news-of-princess-dianas-death/ |access-date=18 August 2024 |website=Radio Times |language=en-GB |archive-date=1 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230401181554/https://www.radiotimes.com/tv/current-affairs/newreader-martyn-lewis-recalls-the-day-he-broke-the-news-of-princess-dianas-death/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On [[BBC World]], [[Nik Gowing]] was on duty within a few hours of the crash for rolling coverage alongside [[Maxine Mawhinney]] and [[George Arney (journalist)|George Arney]].<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?si=ZgT_q5GtKMyojCJ3&v=OEf2zwbbc2w&feature=youtu.be |title=Diana's accident: overnight coverage |date=5 April 2020 |last=David Boothroyd |access-date=18 August 2024 |via=YouTube }}</ref>

On [[ITV (TV network)|ITN]], [[Tim Willcox]] was also on duty for regular news bulletins, announcing the crash at about 1:30{{nbsp}}am. [[Dermot Murnaghan]] would come on the air later, at 4:40am for rolling coverage, later announcing the Princess' death.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?si=VDjG5FdicUaH6Yl4&v=zWzcBE7FfGo&feature=youtu.be |title=Dermot Murnaghan Remembers The Chilling Moment He Had To Announce Princess Diana's Death {{!}} LK |date=31 August 2022 |last=Lorraine |access-date=18 August 2024 |via=YouTube }}</ref>

On [[Sky News]], [[Kay Burley]] announced the death of Diana before 5:15{{nbsp}}am, and stayed for rolling coverage.<ref>{{Cite web |title=How Sky News reported Princess Diana's death 20 years ago |url=https://news.sky.com/story/how-sky-news-reported-princess-dianas-death-20-years-ago-11013173 |access-date=18 August 2024 |website=Sky News |language=en |archive-date=18 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240818022409/https://news.sky.com/story/how-sky-news-reported-princess-dianas-death-20-years-ago-11013173 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the United States, schedules were also scrapped for news coverage. On [[MSNBC]], [[Brian Williams]] was on air around 10{{nbsp}}pm ET, with rolling coverage, before announcing Diana's death at 11:48{{nbsp}}pm ET.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brian Williams Recalls Broadcasting Live on the Night of Princess Diana's Death: 'Rumors Were Flying' |url=https://people.com/royals/princess-diana-death-brian-williams-recalls-live-broadcast/ |access-date=18 August 2024 |website=Peoplemag |language=en |archive-date=18 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240818022409/https://people.com/royals/princess-diana-death-brian-williams-recalls-live-broadcast/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Linden Soles and [[Jim Clancy (journalist)|Jim Clancy]], respectively, were on CNN reporting the car crash and Diana's death, with Soles giving the initial announcement, and Clancy continuing coverage.

== In popular culture ==
* "[[Kärleken är]]", a 1998 song inspired by the aftermath of Diana's death
* ''[[Diana: Last Days of a Princess]]'', a 2007 television docudrama
